[
  {
    "topicTitle": "Git and GitHub Fundamentals",
    "sections": [
      {
        "id": "stage-2-topic-1-section-1",
        "title": "Version Control Fundamentals and the Role of Git",
        "content": "Version control records how source code and related project files change over time. Git is a distributed version control system: each developer can hold a complete local repository containing project history, commits, branches, and references. This differs from a purely centralized model in which most history operations depend on a central server.\n\nA repository tracks files through snapshots represented by commits. Git does not simply store a separate full copy of every file for every commit; its object model and content-addressed storage allow objects to be reused efficiently. Developers use version control to collaborate, review changes, compare releases, recover earlier states, and investigate regressions.\n\nGit and GitHub are related but different. Git is the version control tool running locally or in automation. GitHub is a hosted collaboration platform that provides remote repositories, pull requests, access controls, issues, automation, and review workflows. A team can use Git without GitHub, and GitHub repositories are operated using Git-compatible workflows.\n\nIn a DevOps delivery process, Git history provides traceability from a requirement or defect to a code change, review, automated validation, and release. A reliable team treats commits as auditable change records and protects the repository as a critical engineering asset."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-2-topic-1-section-2",
        "title": "Git Object Model: Blobs, Trees, Commits and References",
        "content": "Git stores repository data using objects. A blob stores file content without representing its filename. A tree records directory structure and maps names and file modes to blobs or other trees. A commit points to a root tree and records metadata such as author, committer, timestamp, message, and parent commit references. A normal commit therefore connects a project snapshot to its history.\n\nGit identifies objects by content-derived object IDs. Modern repositories can use SHA-1 or SHA-256 object formats depending on repository initialization and compatibility requirements. References such as branch names and tags provide human-readable names for commits. HEAD normally identifies the currently checked-out branch or a specific commit in detached HEAD state.\n\nUseful inspection commands include `git cat-file -t <object-id>`, `git cat-file -p <object-id>`, `git log --oneline --graph --decorate --all`, and `git show <commit>`. These commands help engineers inspect the underlying object relationships rather than relying only on the working directory view.\n\nUnderstanding blobs, trees, commits, and references makes operations such as branching, merging, rebasing, tagging, and recovery easier to reason about. When a branch appears to have lost work, the commit may still exist and can often be located through references or the reflog."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-2-topic-1-section-3",
        "title": "Working Tree, Index and Repository State",
        "content": "Git separates a developer's files into three important states: the working tree, the index (also called the staging area), and the committed repository. The working tree contains the files currently present in the checkout. The index records the exact content selected for the next commit. The repository stores committed snapshots and their history.\n\n`git status` compares these states and reports untracked, modified, staged, and other changes. `git add <path>` updates the index with the selected file content. `git commit` creates a commit from the index, not from every change currently visible in the working tree. This distinction allows a developer to create a focused commit even when unrelated edits exist in the same working directory.\n\nFor example, if a developer modifies `OrderService.java` and `README.md`, but only the service change belongs to the current defect fix, the developer can stage the service file alone and inspect the staged patch using `git diff --cached`. The unstaged documentation change remains outside that commit.\n\nUseful commands include `git diff`, `git diff --staged`, `git status --short`, `git add -p`, and `git restore --staged <path>`. Reviewing the staged diff before committing prevents accidental inclusion of debug code, local configuration, generated files, or secrets."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-2-topic-1-section-4",
        "title": "Repository Initialization, Cloning and Remote Setup",
        "content": "A repository can be created locally with `git init` or obtained from an existing server with `git clone <repository-url>`. Initialization creates the repository metadata in the project directory. Cloning downloads the repository data and creates a working checkout, normally configuring the source remote under the name `origin`.\n\nA remote is a named reference to another Git repository. It is not the repository itself and does not automatically synchronize every local change. `git remote -v` displays configured remote URLs. `git remote add origin <url>` adds a remote, while `git remote set-url origin <url>` changes its URL. `git fetch origin` downloads remote references and objects without merging them into the current branch.\n\nA common team workflow is to clone a repository, create a feature branch, make focused commits, push the branch, and open a pull request. Before pushing, developers should verify the remote and branch with `git remote -v` and `git branch -vv`.\n\nRepository URLs may use HTTPS or SSH. HTTPS commonly uses a credential manager or supported token-based authentication. SSH uses a key pair and server-side public-key registration. Teams should use approved authentication methods, avoid embedding credentials in remote URLs, and verify that the remote points to the intended organization and repository."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-2-topic-1-section-5",
        "title": "Git Configuration, Identity and Effective Settings",
        "content": "Git configuration controls behavior at different scopes. System configuration applies broadly to the machine, global configuration normally applies to the current user, and local repository configuration applies only to one repository. Command-level configuration can override settings for a single invocation. The effective value depends on the scope and precedence rules.\n\nSet a developer identity with `git config --global user.name \"Developer Name\"` and `git config --global user.email \"developer@example.com\"`. These values are recorded in commit metadata; they do not authenticate the developer to GitHub. Authentication to a remote server is a separate process.\n\nInspect settings with `git config --list --show-origin`, `git config --get user.name`, and `git config --show-origin --get core.autocrlf`. The origin information is useful when an unexpected value is inherited from a system or repository configuration file.\n\nTeams should standardize settings that affect consistent collaboration, such as line-ending handling, default branch naming, signing requirements, and safe directory behavior. Avoid copying another developer's identity or committing machine-specific configuration accidentally. In CI, configure an explicit automation identity and use narrowly scoped credentials supplied through the platform's secret mechanism."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-2-topic-1-section-6",
        "title": "Commit Creation, Metadata and Atomic Change Design",
        "content": "A commit records the staged snapshot as a new point in repository history. Create a commit with `git commit -m \"Describe the change\"`. A useful commit message explains the intent and context rather than merely repeating a filename or saying that something was updated.\n\nA good commit is focused and reviewable. It should represent one logical change, such as fixing a validation defect or adding a specific API capability. Mixing unrelated refactoring, generated files, formatting changes, and functional changes makes review and rollback harder. Before committing, inspect `git status` and `git diff --cached` to confirm exactly what will be recorded.\n\nCommit metadata includes author and committer information, timestamps, the commit message, the tree reference, and parent references. Amendments and rebases can rewrite commit identities, so teams should agree on how to handle already-published commits. Avoid rewriting shared history unless the team workflow explicitly permits it.\n\nIn production engineering, atomic commits improve traceability. When a deployment introduces a regression, focused commits help identify the change responsible. They also make cherry-picking, revert operations, release notes, and incident investigation more predictable."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-2-topic-1-section-7",
        "title": "Branch References, HEAD and Detached Checkout",
        "content": "A Git branch is a movable reference to a commit, not a separate copy of the project. Creating a branch gives developers an independent line of development while sharing the underlying repository objects. `git branch` lists local branches, `git switch -c feature/order-validation` creates and checks out a new branch, and `git switch main` changes the current branch.\n\nHEAD normally points to the current branch reference. When a specific commit, tag, or remote-tracking reference is checked out directly, Git may enter detached HEAD state. Commits created in that state are not automatically attached to a named branch. They can be retained by creating a branch with `git switch -c recovery/experiment` before switching away.\n\nRemote-tracking references such as `origin/main` represent the last fetched view of a remote branch. They are updated by operations such as `git fetch`, not by editing the remote directly. `git branch -vv` shows local branch tracking relationships and ahead/behind information.\n\nEngineers should use clear branch names and remove obsolete local branches after confirming their work is integrated. Before deleting a branch, verify its commits are merged or safely preserved. Understanding references and HEAD is essential when diagnosing missing commits or an unexpected checkout state."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-2-topic-1-section-8",
        "title": "Ignore Rules, Tracked Files and Repository Hygiene",
        "content": "Git ignore rules prevent selected untracked files from being proposed for addition. A repository-level `.gitignore` is commonly used for build output, temporary files, local IDE metadata, test reports, and machine-specific artifacts. Patterns can target file names, directories, extensions, and paths; a leading slash anchors a pattern relative to the ignore file, while a trailing slash targets directories.\n\nUse `git check-ignore -v <path>` to identify which rule ignores a file. Ignore rules do not remove files that are already tracked. If a tracked generated file must stop being versioned, remove it from the index while preserving the local copy with `git rm --cached <path>`, then commit the change.\n\nRepository hygiene requires deliberate decisions about generated artifacts. Build outputs such as `target/`, `build/`, and temporary coverage reports are usually regenerated and should not be committed unless the project has a specific reason. Conversely, lockfiles, migrations, deployment manifests, and required build configuration often belong in version control.\n\nNever rely on `.gitignore` as a secret-protection mechanism. A credential can be committed before an ignore rule is added, and removing it from the latest commit does not erase it from earlier history. If a secret is exposed, revoke or rotate it immediately and follow the organization's history-cleanup and incident-response process."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-2-topic-1-section-9",
        "title": "Inspecting History, Diffs and Change Provenance",
        "content": "Git provides several views for understanding how a repository reached its current state. `git log --oneline --graph --decorate --all` summarizes commit topology. `git show <commit>` displays a commit and its patch. `git diff <base>..<target>` compares two commit references, while `git diff --` compares working-tree changes with the index and `git diff --cached` compares staged content with HEAD.\n\nUse `git blame <file>` to identify the commit associated with each line in a file. Blame is a navigation aid, not proof of who is responsible for a defect; code may have been moved, copied, generated, or changed by later commits. `git log --follow -- <file>` can help trace a file across many renames.\n\nDuring an incident, engineers can compare the deployed release tag with the previous known-good tag, inspect commits between them, and map suspicious changes to pull requests or tickets. `git diff --stat` gives a compact summary, while a full patch review exposes exact modifications.\n\nA reliable investigation records the commit IDs, branch or tag names, comparison range, and relevant deployment version. This creates reproducible evidence for debugging, release review, and audit activities."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-2-topic-1-section-10",
        "title": "GitHub Remote Collaboration and Repository Permissions",
        "content": "GitHub adds hosted collaboration capabilities around Git repositories. Teams use organizations, repositories, teams, and role-based permissions to control who can read, write, administer, or manage repository settings. Permission design should follow least privilege: developers receive the access needed for their work, while administrative privileges remain limited.\n\nA local Git operation and a GitHub web action may enforce different controls. For example, a developer may create a local commit without contacting GitHub, but pushing that commit requires successful remote authentication and authorization. Repository rules, branch protection, organization policy, and required checks can reject a push or prevent a pull request from merging.\n\nUse `git remote -v` to verify the remote destination and `git ls-remote origin` to inspect advertised remote references when access permits. On GitHub, review repository access, team membership, deploy keys, GitHub Apps, and machine-user credentials as separate access paths.\n\nFor production repositories, prefer organization-managed teams, short-lived or narrowly scoped credentials where supported, and auditable service identities for automation. Remove access promptly when roles change, and periodically review external collaborators and unused credentials."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-2-topic-1-section-11",
        "title": "Repository Integrity, Secret Safety and Signed Commits",
        "content": "Repository security includes protecting credentials, controlling write access, reviewing dependencies and changes, and maintaining trustworthy history. Passwords, personal access tokens, private keys, cloud credentials, certificates, and production connection strings must not be committed to source control. Store sensitive values in an approved secret manager or CI/CD secret store and inject them at runtime.\n\nIf a credential is committed, deleting the line in a later commit is not sufficient. The credential may remain in earlier commits, forks, clones, logs, and caches. Revoke or rotate the exposed credential first, investigate its use, and then follow the organization's approved history-removal procedure. Secret scanning can detect known patterns, but it does not replace secure design and access controls.\n\nSigned commits and signed tags can provide cryptographic evidence that a commit or release tag was signed by a trusted key. Teams must configure accepted signing methods, distribute trust information, and define how verification failures are handled. A signature proves possession of a signing key, not that the code is safe or correct.\n\nProduction repositories should combine least-privilege access, protected branches, required reviews, automated secret scanning, dependency checks, and audit logging. Security controls should be tested and monitored rather than assumed to be active."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-2-topic-1-section-12",
        "title": "Repository Recovery with Reflog, Reset and Revert",
        "content": "Git provides different recovery mechanisms for different repository states. `git reflog` records recent movements of local references such as HEAD and can help locate commits after a reset, branch change, or rebase. Reflog entries are local and expire according to repository maintenance settings, so they should not be treated as a permanent backup.\n\n`git restore <path>` can discard working-tree changes, while `git restore --staged <path>` removes a path from the index. These commands can discard work, so inspect the target and preserve valuable changes before using them. `git reset` moves the current branch reference and can also change the index or working tree depending on whether `--soft`, `--mixed`, or `--hard` is used. `--hard` can destroy uncommitted changes.\n\n`git revert <commit>` creates a new commit that reverses the effect of an earlier commit. It is generally safer for shared branches because it preserves published history. Reset is more appropriate for carefully controlled local history changes before publication.\n\nFor recovery, first inspect `git status`, create a safety branch or copy when appropriate, inspect `git reflog`, and identify the exact commit or file state to restore. In a production incident, prefer a reviewed revert on the shared branch and validate the resulting build and tests."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-2-topic-1-section-13",
        "title": "End-to-End Git Workflow and Production Collaboration Lab",
        "content": "Use a realistic workflow to connect local Git operations with hosted collaboration. Clone a team repository, inspect its remotes and default branch, update the local view with `git fetch`, and create a feature branch from the approved base. Make a small, focused code change, inspect the working tree, stage only the intended files, and review the staged diff before committing.\n\nPush the branch with `git push -u origin feature/order-validation`. Open a GitHub pull request that explains the business problem, implementation, testing performed, operational impact, and any migration or configuration requirements. Reviewers inspect the patch, discuss changes, and verify automated checks. Required branch rules should prevent merging until the defined approvals and checks succeed.\n\nAfter merge, update the local base branch and remove the feature branch when it is no longer needed. For a release, identify the exact commit, create an approved version tag, and ensure the build pipeline records the source revision used to produce the artifact.\n\nThe lab is complete when the change is traceable from commit to pull request and release, the automated checks pass, no secrets or unrelated files are included, and the team can identify how to revert the change. Record the commands, commit ID, pull request, test evidence, and any operational follow-up."
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "topicTitle": "Repository Management",
    "sections": [
      {
        "id": "stage-2-topic-2-section-1",
        "title": "Repository Lifecycle and Administrative Responsibilities",
        "content": "Repository management covers the complete administrative lifecycle of a source-code repository: planning, creation, configuration, collaboration, maintenance, governance, archival, and eventual retirement. A repository is not merely a directory containing files. It is an engineering asset that holds source history, documentation, automation definitions, configuration, and evidence of software changes.\n\nA repository owner or administrator is responsible for keeping the repository discoverable, accessible to the correct teams, configured consistently, and aligned with organizational policies. Responsibilities include selecting an appropriate visibility model, assigning maintainers, configuring repository metadata, maintaining documentation, reviewing integrations, and ensuring that operational ownership is clear.\n\nA repository should have a defined purpose and lifecycle. For example, an application repository may contain application source code and tests, while infrastructure definitions may live in a separate repository with different access requirements. The decision should consider ownership, release independence, dependency boundaries, and security classification.\n\nIn DevOps environments, repository administration directly affects delivery reliability. Incorrect visibility, missing ownership, abandoned credentials, or unmaintained automation can create production risks. Teams should document repository ownership, critical contacts, expected maintenance, and retirement conditions."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-2-topic-2-section-2",
        "title": "Creating Repositories and Selecting the Correct Repository Model",
        "content": "GitHub repositories can be created through the web interface, GitHub CLI, or supported APIs. When creating a repository, administrators choose its name, description, visibility, initialization options, and organization or owner. The repository name should be recognizable, stable, and consistent with organizational naming conventions.\n\nA new repository can be initialized with a README, license, or `.gitignore` template. These options create useful baseline files, but teams should avoid initializing a remote repository with unrelated starter content when they already have a local repository containing its own history. Two independently initialized histories can require additional reconciliation before the first push.\n\nGitHub CLI supports repository creation with commands such as `gh repo create organization/service-api --private --source=. --remote=origin --push`. The exact options should be checked against the installed CLI version and organizational authentication policy. API-based creation can be useful for standardized provisioning through internal automation.\n\nChoose repository visibility deliberately. Public repositories expose content to the public internet, private repositories restrict access to authorized identities, and internal visibility is available in supported GitHub Enterprise configurations. The selected model must match data classification, intellectual-property rules, and organizational policy."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-2-topic-2-section-3",
        "title": "Repository Metadata, Naming and Discoverability",
        "content": "Repository metadata helps engineers understand what a repository contains, who owns it, and how it should be used. Important metadata includes the repository name, description, website URL, topics, license, default branch, and ownership information. A meaningful description should explain the system or component rather than repeat the repository name.\n\nTopics are searchable labels that classify repositories by technology or responsibility, such as `java`, `spring-boot`, `terraform`, `platform-engineering`, or `observability`. Use a controlled naming vocabulary where possible. Excessive or inconsistent labels reduce discoverability and make repository catalogs difficult to maintain.\n\nA useful README should explain the repository purpose, prerequisites, local setup, build and test commands, configuration requirements, deployment process, and links to operational documentation. It should distinguish safe example configuration from real credentials and explain where required secrets are obtained.\n\nFor large organizations, consistent metadata enables repository discovery, ownership reporting, compliance inventory, and automation. Teams can periodically identify repositories with missing descriptions, unclear owners, outdated documentation, or incorrect topics. Good metadata reduces onboarding time and prevents engineers from modifying the wrong project."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-2-topic-2-section-4",
        "title": "Repository Layout, Documentation and Contribution Standards",
        "content": "Repository layout should make the project understandable without requiring undocumented knowledge from its original developers. Application repositories commonly separate source code, automated tests, build configuration, deployment definitions, documentation, and supporting scripts according to the technology and architecture.\n\nCommon root-level files include `README.md`, `LICENSE`, `.gitignore`, `CONTRIBUTING.md`, and security or support policy documents. A `CODEOWNERS` file can identify responsible reviewers for selected paths on supported GitHub plans and configurations. These files serve different purposes and should not be treated as interchangeable administrative settings.\n\nContribution documentation should describe prerequisites, local development setup, formatting and test commands, supported runtime versions, and the expected process for proposing changes. A security policy should explain how vulnerabilities are reported privately. A license should be selected according to legal and organizational guidance rather than copied without review.\n\nRepository standards should be practical and enforceable. For example, a Java service repository might document the required JDK, Maven commands, environment variables, database setup, and test execution. Consistent documentation helps new engineers contribute safely and helps automation run the project reproducibly."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-2-topic-2-section-5",
        "title": "Remote Repository Configuration and URL Administration",
        "content": "A remote connects a local Git repository to another repository hosted on a server. Common remote names include `origin` for the primary remote and `upstream` for the canonical source repository in a fork-based workflow. Remote configuration is stored locally and does not itself grant permission to access the destination.\n\nInspect remote configuration with `git remote -v`. Add a remote using `git remote add origin <url>`, rename one using `git remote rename <old> <new>`, and update its address with `git remote set-url origin <new-url>`. Verify the destination before pushing, particularly when maintaining separate development, migration, or archived repositories.\n\nHTTPS and SSH are common transport methods. HTTPS authentication may use a credential manager or an approved token mechanism. SSH authentication uses a private key held by the client and a registered public key or supported deploy-key configuration. Credentials must never be embedded directly in scripts or committed repository files.\n\nRepository administrators should communicate canonical clone URLs and migration changes to consumers. If a repository is transferred or renamed, verify redirects, automation references, deployment configuration, documentation links, webhooks, and external integrations. A remote URL change can affect many downstream systems even when the Git history itself remains intact."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-2-topic-2-section-6",
        "title": "Repository Access Model, Teams and Permission Administration",
        "content": "Repository access determines which identities can view or administer repository resources. GitHub supports role-based permissions, with roles and capabilities varying by account type and enterprise configuration. Administrators should understand the difference between read, triage, write, maintain, and administrative capabilities rather than granting broad access by default.\n\nOrganization teams provide a scalable way to manage access across multiple repositories. Instead of adding individual collaborators repeatedly, an organization can maintain teams that represent engineering groups, platform operations, security, or service ownership. Repository access can then be assigned to the appropriate team using the least privilege required.\n\nAccess reviews should include direct collaborators, organization teams, outside collaborators, deploy keys, GitHub Apps, machine identities, and other integration identities. Removing a person from one team may not remove access granted through another team or a separate integration.\n\nFor production repositories, define who can administer settings, manage integrations, and change sensitive configuration. Review access when employees change roles, projects are retired, or vendors complete their engagement. Keep a documented owner and escalation path so access problems can be resolved without granting uncontrolled permissions."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-2-topic-2-section-7",
        "title": "Repository Features, Settings and Operational Configuration",
        "content": "Repository settings control how GitHub features behave for a particular project. Depending on the repository and organization configuration, administrators may enable or disable issues, projects, discussions, wiki pages, downloads, and other collaboration capabilities. The correct configuration depends on how the team plans to communicate and manage work.\n\nSettings should be intentional. For example, a team using an external issue tracker may choose not to use GitHub Issues for primary work management, while another team may rely on issues and project boards for planning. Enabling several overlapping systems without ownership can split information and make operational tracking unreliable.\n\nAdministrators should review repository visibility, default branch configuration, merge-related settings, archive state, interaction restrictions, and available security features. Organization-level policies may override or restrict repository-level choices, so a setting visible in the interface may not be freely changeable by a repository administrator.\n\nDocument important configuration decisions and review them after repository ownership or delivery processes change. For critical repositories, maintain a configuration checklist so newly created repositories receive consistent baseline settings and unnecessary features are not enabled accidentally."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-2-topic-2-section-8",
        "title": "Fork-Based Repository Ownership and Upstream Synchronization",
        "content": "A fork is a separate repository created from another repository, commonly used when a contributor does not have direct write access to the original project. The fork provides an independent place to develop changes while retaining a relationship to the source repository.\n\nIn a common workflow, `origin` points to the contributor's fork and `upstream` points to the canonical repository. Developers fetch updates from upstream, integrate the approved base branch into their working branch, and push their changes to origin. For example, `git remote add upstream <canonical-url>` configures the source, while `git fetch upstream` retrieves its current references.\n\nForks are useful for open-source contributions, external collaboration, and isolated experimentation. However, they introduce additional repository copies that may contain outdated code, sensitive history, or unmaintained branches. Organizations should define policies for internal forks, visibility, synchronization, and retirement.\n\nWhen synchronizing a fork, inspect the upstream changes and choose a suitable merge or rebase strategy according to the team's history policy. Validate the resulting code and tests before proposing changes to the canonical repository. Never assume that a fork automatically receives security fixes or configuration changes from its source."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-2-topic-2-section-9",
        "title": "Repository Integrations, Applications and Webhook Administration",
        "content": "Repositories often connect to external services for CI/CD, security scanning, issue management, notifications, deployment, and reporting. Integrations may use GitHub Apps, OAuth applications, deploy keys, or webhooks. Each mechanism has a different permission and event model, so administrators should understand which identity is accessing the repository and what it can do.\n\nGitHub Apps can receive narrowly scoped permissions and subscribe to supported events. Webhooks deliver event payloads to configured endpoints, allowing external systems to react to repository activity. A webhook configuration should use an approved HTTPS endpoint, a secret for signature validation where supported, and operational monitoring for delivery failures.\n\nReview installed applications and integrations periodically. Remove unused applications, reduce excessive permissions, and confirm that each integration has an accountable owner. A forgotten integration can retain access long after the original project or service has changed.\n\nFor production integrations, record the purpose, permissions, owner, endpoint, authentication method, and failure-handling process. Validate webhook signatures, avoid logging sensitive payload data unnecessarily, and ensure that external systems handle duplicate delivery or temporary failures safely."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-2-topic-2-section-10",
        "title": "Repository Governance, Compliance and Administrative Auditing",
        "content": "Repository governance defines the minimum standards required for safe and consistent source-code management. It can cover ownership, visibility, approved integrations, license requirements, documentation, security scanning, retention, and access review. Governance should be proportional to repository risk and should not impose unnecessary controls on low-risk experimental work.\n\nOrganizations may use enterprise policies, repository templates, rulesets, security configurations, and automated checks to apply consistent standards. Repository administrators should understand which controls are centrally managed and which are configurable locally. A local setting should not be assumed to override an organization-wide restriction.\n\nAudit activities should examine repository ownership, collaborator access, visibility, installed applications, deploy keys, webhooks, security alerts, and administrative changes. GitHub audit log capabilities depend on the account and plan. Where audit logs are available, retain and review them according to organizational policy.\n\nA practical governance process assigns an accountable owner, records the repository's business purpose, defines the required protection level, and schedules periodic reviews. Exceptions should have a documented reason, an approving authority, and an expiration or reassessment date."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-2-topic-2-section-11",
        "title": "Repository Maintenance, Archival, Transfer and Retirement",
        "content": "Repository maintenance keeps project metadata, documentation, integrations, and access configuration accurate over time. Routine work includes reviewing inactive collaborators, removing obsolete integrations, updating repository descriptions, checking security alerts, and confirming that the repository still has an active owner.\n\nArchiving a repository makes its historical content available in a read-only state on GitHub, subject to platform behavior and permissions. Archival is useful when a project is no longer actively developed but its history must remain accessible. Before archiving, verify that active consumers have migrated, required artifacts are retained, and operational dependencies have been addressed.\n\nRepository transfer changes the repository's owner or organization. Before a transfer, check destination policies, permissions, integrations, automation, links, package references, and billing or compliance requirements. Renaming and transferring can affect clone URLs, webhook configurations, CI pipelines, badges, documentation, and external consumers.\n\nDeletion is a high-impact action and should follow an approved retention and recovery process. Confirm ownership approval, backup requirements, dependent systems, and legal or audit obligations before deleting. For important repositories, preserve a recoverable copy or approved archive before retirement."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-2-topic-2-section-12",
        "title": "Repository Administration Automation and Production Operations Lab",
        "content": "This lab applies repository administration practices to a service repository used by a DevOps team. Begin by identifying the repository owner, purpose, visibility requirement, default branch, and required collaborators. Create or inspect the repository using the GitHub interface or approved GitHub CLI workflow, and verify its canonical remote URL.\n\nConfigure descriptive metadata, topics, README documentation, license information where applicable, and contribution guidance. Assign access through the correct organization teams, review direct collaborators, and identify integrations that the service requires. Do not create broad permissions simply to make an automation workflow succeed.\n\nPrepare an administration checklist that records repository settings, access owners, external applications, webhook endpoints, security controls, and review frequency. Use `gh repo view organization/service-api --json name,description,url,visibility,isArchived` where supported by the installed GitHub CLI version to inspect selected repository metadata. Confirm the actual available fields using the CLI help if a field is unsupported.\n\nFinally, simulate a repository maintenance review: identify an unused integration, an inactive collaborator, outdated documentation, and a repository that should be archived rather than deleted. Document the proposed action, approval requirement, validation evidence, and rollback or recovery considerations.\n\nThe lab is complete when the repository has a clear owner, correct visibility, least-privilege access, useful metadata, documented integrations, and an approved maintenance plan. No destructive archival, transfer, or deletion action should be performed as part of this exercise."
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "topicTitle": "Branching Strategy",
    "sections": [
      {
        "id": "stage-2-topic-3-section-1",
        "title": "Branching Strategy Fundamentals and Engineering Objectives",
        "content": "A branching strategy defines how developers create, use, synchronize, review, and retire branches while multiple people work on the same repository. A branch provides an independent line of development, allowing a developer to make changes without immediately modifying the shared integration branch. A strategy is more than a naming convention: it establishes how work moves from development to integration, testing, release, and production. A good strategy reduces accidental interference, makes changes traceable, and gives the team a predictable path for delivering software.\n\nThe correct strategy depends on team size, deployment frequency, release requirements, compliance obligations, and the ability to deploy incomplete functionality safely. A team delivering small changes continuously may benefit from short-lived feature branches and frequent integration. A product with scheduled releases and strict maintenance requirements may need release branches. Long-lived branches can be justified for supported product versions, but they create synchronization costs and increase the risk of integration conflicts.\n\nBranching decisions should be evaluated using practical engineering outcomes: how quickly changes reach the main branch, how often conflicts occur, how easy rollback is, and whether every deployable artifact can be traced to a commit. Avoid selecting GitFlow, trunk-based development, or another model simply because it is popular. Choose the least complicated workflow that satisfies the product's delivery and support requirements."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-2-topic-3-section-2",
        "title": "Branch Lifecycle, Ownership, and Naming Conventions",
        "content": "A branch should have a clear purpose, an identifiable owner or responsible team, and a defined lifecycle. A typical feature branch is created from the current integration branch, receives a focused set of commits, is tested through a pull request, and is deleted after successful integration. A release branch may remain active longer because it receives stabilization fixes, while a hotfix branch exists to address an urgent production defect. These different lifecycles should be explicit so that developers do not accidentally treat every branch as permanent.\n\nUse consistent, searchable names that communicate the work type and tracking reference. Examples include feature/482-add-user-search, bugfix/731-fix-null-response, release/2.4.0, and hotfix/2.4.1. A repository may adopt another convention, but it should document allowed prefixes, separators, ticket references, and restrictions. Avoid names such as test, temp, new-branch, or final because they do not explain ownership or purpose.\n\nBranch names are not security boundaries and should not contain credentials, customer information, or confidential incident details. Establish rules for who may create release and maintenance branches, how stale branches are identified, and when deletion is permitted. A branch should not be deleted while it is the only recoverable reference to unmerged work. Before cleanup, verify its pull request, merge status, and any required release or audit references."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-2-topic-3-section-3",
        "title": "Trunk-Based Development and Short-Lived Feature Branches",
        "content": "Trunk-based development centers collaboration on one shared mainline, commonly named main. Developers integrate small changes frequently instead of maintaining separate development branches for long periods. Some teams commit directly to the mainline under strict automated controls; others use very short-lived feature branches and pull requests. The essential principle is that branches should live for a short time and changes should be integrated before they drift significantly from the mainline.\n\nA practical workflow is to update the local main branch, create a small feature branch, implement one focused change, run local tests, push the branch, and open a pull request. Automated checks validate formatting, compilation, unit tests, security rules, and other repository requirements. Reviewers examine the change, and the branch is merged promptly after approval and successful checks. Developers then update their local branches and begin the next change from the latest mainline.\n\nIncomplete functionality should be managed with techniques such as feature flags, configuration controls, or backward-compatible implementation steps. A feature flag allows code to be integrated without exposing the feature to all users. It must have an owner, a safe default, appropriate access controls, and a removal plan. Trunk-based development works particularly well when automated tests, fast feedback, observability, and deployment practices are mature. Without those controls, frequent integration can expose unstable changes."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-2-topic-3-section-4",
        "title": "GitFlow Branching Model and Release-Oriented Workflows",
        "content": "GitFlow is a branching model designed around structured releases and distinct development, release, and production-support activities. Its traditional model uses a long-lived main branch for production history and a develop branch for integrating upcoming work. Feature branches are created from develop and merged back after completion. A release branch is created when the team begins stabilizing a planned release. After validation, the release is merged into main and the development line, with a version tag identifying the released commit. Hotfix branches are created from the production line to address urgent defects.\n\nThis model provides explicit places for release preparation and production fixes. It can be useful when a product has scheduled release trains, multiple supported versions, formal stabilization periods, or coordinated testing requirements. However, every additional long-lived branch introduces synchronization work. Developers must understand which branch is authoritative, where a fix must be merged, and how changes flow back into future releases.\n\nGitFlow is not automatically the best choice for every repository. Teams deploying many times each day may find its branch structure unnecessarily complex. If it is adopted, document the purpose of each branch, permitted merge directions, release ownership, and hotfix propagation rules. Automate branch creation, validation, tagging, and cleanup where practical, and regularly review whether the model still matches the team's delivery process."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-2-topic-3-section-5",
        "title": "GitHub Flow and Continuous Delivery Branching",
        "content": "GitHub Flow is a lightweight workflow in which the main branch is expected to remain in a releasable state. A developer creates a descriptive branch from main, makes a focused change, pushes commits, and opens a pull request. The pull request provides a shared location for discussion, review, automated checks, and approval. Once the required checks pass and the change is approved, the branch is merged into main. The updated main branch can then trigger a deployment pipeline.\n\nThis workflow is straightforward because it avoids maintaining a separate permanent develop branch. It encourages small changes and makes the pull request the central integration boundary. Teams can connect branch creation and pull request events to CI pipelines that run tests, build artifacts, scan dependencies, and publish preview environments. Deployment policies can then promote the same verified artifact through appropriate environments.\n\nA releasable main branch requires discipline. Incomplete features should be hidden behind controlled feature flags or implemented through backward-compatible steps. Required checks must be reliable, and the team should have a clear process for reverting a defective change. GitHub Flow is suitable for many web applications and services with continuous delivery, but it still needs release controls where production deployment requires manual authorization, scheduled windows, or regulatory evidence."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-2-topic-3-section-6",
        "title": "Feature, Bugfix, Release, and Hotfix Branch Responsibilities",
        "content": "Different branch types support different engineering activities. A feature branch isolates development of a planned capability. A bugfix branch isolates correction of a known defect that has not necessarily reached production. A release branch stabilizes a specific version by accepting only approved fixes, documentation updates, and release metadata changes. A hotfix branch addresses an urgent production issue and should be based on the exact production version that needs correction.\n\nThe starting point matters. Creating a hotfix from the latest development branch can accidentally include unrelated changes that are not approved for production. Instead, identify the deployed commit or release tag, create the hotfix from that reference, make the smallest safe correction, and validate it against the affected production version. After releasing the fix, propagate the correction into the active development line and any maintained release branches. This prevents the same defect from reappearing in a later release.\n\nDefine who can create and merge each branch type. Release and hotfix branches may require additional approvals, change records, security checks, or deployment authorization. Keep each branch focused; do not combine unrelated feature work with emergency repair work. Record the originating issue, target version, validation evidence, and final merge references so that the branch history supports incident investigation and future maintenance."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-2-topic-3-section-7",
        "title": "Feature Flags, Incremental Delivery, and Branch Lifetime",
        "content": "Long-running feature branches often become difficult to integrate because the mainline continues changing while the feature remains isolated. The longer the branch lives, the more commits it may need to absorb, and the more likely it is to contain incompatible assumptions about shared code, database schemas, APIs, or configuration. Smaller branches reduce this integration gap and make failures easier to identify.\n\nFeature flags provide another way to separate code integration from feature exposure. A team can merge an incomplete but safe implementation into main while keeping the new behavior disabled for ordinary users. Flags may support internal testing, controlled rollout, percentage-based exposure, or rapid disabling when monitoring identifies a problem. A flag must be evaluated carefully: an incorrectly configured flag can expose unfinished behavior or create different runtime paths that are not adequately tested.\n\nFlags should not become permanent substitutes for removing completed work from branches. Assign ownership, define the intended rollout condition, test both enabled and disabled behavior where necessary, and remove obsolete flags after rollout. Avoid creating flags for every trivial code path. Combine short-lived branches, small pull requests, backward-compatible changes, and temporary flags to reduce the need for long-running branches while keeping production behavior controlled."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-2-topic-3-section-8",
        "title": "Creating Branches, Tracking Remote Branches, and Safe Pushes",
        "content": "Git supports local branches and remote-tracking references. A local branch is a movable reference used for local development. A remote-tracking reference, such as origin/main, records the last fetched state of a branch from the remote repository; it is not itself a live connection to the server. Developers should fetch remote updates before making decisions based on the latest shared branch state.\n\nUseful commands include `git branch` to list local branches, `git branch -a` to include remote-tracking references, `git switch -c feature/482-search` to create and switch to a branch, and `git push -u origin feature/482-search` to publish it and configure upstream tracking. After upstream tracking is configured, ordinary `git push` and `git pull` can use the configured remote branch. Use `git branch -vv` to inspect tracking relationships and ahead/behind information.\n\nBefore pushing, inspect the current branch with `git status --short --branch` and review the commits with `git log --oneline`. Avoid force-pushing shared branches because it can overwrite commits published by teammates. If history rewriting is necessary on a personal branch, understand the impact and prefer `git push --force-with-lease` over an unconditional force push. Repository permissions and branch protection should prevent unauthorized updates to important shared branches."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-2-topic-3-section-9",
        "title": "Keeping Feature Branches Synchronized with the Integration Branch",
        "content": "A feature branch can become outdated while other developers merge changes into the shared integration branch. Synchronization brings relevant upstream changes into the working branch and helps discover integration problems before the pull request is merged. First fetch the latest remote references with `git fetch origin`, then inspect the difference between the feature branch and its base. The team should choose a consistent synchronization approach so that developers do not unintentionally rewrite shared history.\n\nMerging the latest base branch into a feature branch creates a merge commit when necessary and preserves the existing branch history. Rebasing replays the feature commits on top of a newer base and produces a more linear history, but it changes commit identifiers. Rebasing is generally appropriate for a developer's private feature branch before it is shared or when repository policy explicitly allows it. Avoid rebasing commits that other developers have already based work on unless the team coordinates the rewrite.\n\nAfter synchronization, resolve any conflicts deliberately, run relevant tests, and inspect the resulting history. Commands such as `git log --graph --oneline --decorate --all` help visualize branch relationships. A synchronization policy should explain when to merge, when to rebase, how to handle force pushes, and how frequently long-running branches must be updated."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-2-topic-3-section-10",
        "title": "Branch Strategy Integration with Pull Requests and Protection Rules",
        "content": "A branching strategy must work together with pull request policy and repository protection settings. The strategy defines where work starts and where it should be integrated. Pull request rules define how proposed changes are reviewed and validated. Branch protection or repository rulesets enforce requirements such as required approvals, successful status checks, restricted direct pushes, signed commits, or restrictions on deleting and force-pushing important branches.\n\nFor example, a team may allow developers to create feature branches freely but require every change to main to pass a build, unit tests, dependency scanning, and at least one independent approval. A release branch may have stricter permissions and require approval from a release owner. A hotfix path may permit an expedited review process, but it should still record the incident, validation evidence, and authorization required by the organization.\n\nConfigure policies to match the chosen model. Do not require a develop branch if the repository uses a simple mainline workflow. Do not allow direct pushes to a protected release branch merely because a release is urgent. Test rules with representative user roles and pull request scenarios. Document how exceptions are approved, audited, and removed so that temporary bypasses do not silently become permanent weaknesses."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-2-topic-3-section-11",
        "title": "Branch-Aware CI/CD Pipelines and Environment Promotion",
        "content": "Continuous integration pipelines should respond to the branch model instead of applying unrelated release behavior to every branch. Feature branches commonly run fast validation such as formatting, static analysis, unit tests, build checks, and security scanning. Pull request pipelines can add integration tests, code coverage checks, preview deployment, and policy validation. The main branch pipeline may publish a versioned artifact and deploy it to a development or production environment according to the organization's release controls.\n\nRelease branches may require stabilization checks, regression suites, compatibility validation, and candidate artifact creation. Hotfix branches should run focused tests for the defect as well as the relevant regression suite. The pipeline should record the source commit, branch or tag, build identifier, artifact digest, and test results. These records help prove which source revision produced a deployed artifact.\n\nAvoid building different binaries for each environment when the delivery process requires promotion of one verified artifact. Build once, store the immutable artifact, and promote that same artifact through controlled environments. Branch filters and workflow conditions must be reviewed carefully: an overly broad trigger can deploy an unreviewed branch, while an overly narrow filter can leave important changes untested. Protect deployment credentials and use environment approvals for sensitive targets."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-2-topic-3-section-12",
        "title": "Branch Cleanup, Stale Branch Detection, and Repository Governance",
        "content": "Branch cleanup reduces repository clutter and makes active work easier to identify. After a feature branch has been merged and any required checks are complete, it can normally be deleted. GitHub can automatically delete head branches after pull request merges when the repository setting is enabled. Local developers can refresh remote-tracking references with `git fetch --prune` and inspect local branches with `git branch --merged` before deleting obsolete work.\n\nDo not assume that every branch that appears old is safe to remove. A branch may contain unmerged work, support a maintained release, or be required for an active incident investigation. Before deleting it, check whether it is merged into the intended target, whether an open pull request references it, and whether a release or maintenance policy requires retention. Use `git branch --contains <commit>` and pull request history when investigating where a commit is present.\n\nRepository governance should define stale-branch thresholds, ownership expectations, naming rules, retention requirements, and cleanup responsibility. Automation can identify inactive branches and notify owners, but destructive deletion should follow a documented policy. Protect important release references and tags, and ensure that required audit records remain available even after temporary working branches are removed."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-2-topic-3-section-13",
        "title": "Production Branching Workflow, Decision Matrix, and Team Exercise",
        "content": "A production-ready branching workflow should be explainable from the moment a developer starts a change until the resulting artifact is deployed and traceable. For a small continuously delivered service, a practical sequence is: update main, create a short-lived feature branch, make a focused change, push it, open a pull request, pass automated checks, obtain review approval, merge, and allow the controlled pipeline to build and promote the resulting artifact. For a product with scheduled releases, the team may additionally create a release branch, restrict accepted changes, validate a release candidate, tag the approved commit, and merge necessary fixes back into the development line.\n\nFor an emergency production defect, identify the deployed version first. Create a hotfix branch from the relevant production tag or commit, implement the smallest safe correction, run focused and regression tests, obtain the required emergency approval, and deploy through the authorized pipeline. After deployment, propagate the fix to active release and development branches. Confirm that the corrected behavior is observable and that the original defect has a regression test.\n\nTeam exercise: choose a branching model for a service that deploys several times per week but also maintains two supported release versions. Define branch names, branch starting points, pull request requirements, CI checks, release tagging, hotfix propagation, and cleanup rules. Draw the commit flow for a normal feature and an emergency fix. Review the design for unnecessary long-lived branches, missing validation, ambiguous merge direction, and unsafe production access. The final policy should be short enough for developers to follow and detailed enough for release engineers to enforce."
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "topicTitle": "Commit History",
    "sections": [
      {
        "id": "stage-2-topic-4-section-1",
        "title": "Git Commit Fundamentals and Snapshot Model",
        "content": "A Git commit records a version of the repository at a particular point in its history. It contains a reference to a root tree representing the tracked directory structure, metadata about the author and committer, a commit message, and a reference to one or more parent commits. A normal commit usually has one parent, while a merge commit can have multiple parents. The commit identifies the repository content through Git's object model rather than storing a simple full copy of every file each time.\n\nGit uses content-addressed objects. File contents are stored as blob objects, directory structures are represented by tree objects, and commits reference trees and parent commits. Git can reuse unchanged objects, making repository history efficient. Modern Git repositories commonly use SHA-1 object identifiers, with support for SHA-256 repositories in appropriate configurations. An object identifier helps identify an object; it should not be treated as a secret or as proof that the code is trustworthy.\n\nA commit is created from the content in the index, also called the staging area. This means a commit does not automatically include every modified file in the working directory. Developers should understand the difference between working-tree changes, staged changes, and committed history. This separation allows a focused commit to contain only the intended changes, even when other unrelated work exists locally."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-2-topic-4-section-2",
        "title": "Working Tree, Staging Area, and Commit Creation Workflow",
        "content": "Git separates local changes into three important states: the working tree, the staging area, and the committed repository history. The working tree contains the files currently checked out and any local modifications. The staging area, also called the index, records the exact content selected for the next commit. The repository history contains commits that have already been created. Understanding these states prevents accidental inclusion of unrelated changes.\n\nA typical workflow begins with `git status` to inspect repository state. Use `git diff` to review unstaged modifications and `git diff --staged` to review what is already selected for the next commit. Add a specific file with `git add src/service.ts`, or interactively stage portions of a file using `git add -p`. Then create the commit with `git commit -m \"Add service health endpoint\"`. Finally, inspect the result with `git show --stat --oneline HEAD`.\n\nAvoid using `git add .` without reviewing the resulting staged changes, especially in repositories containing generated files, local configuration, credentials, or unrelated work. Before committing, check for accidentally staged secrets, temporary files, build output, and debug changes. A deliberate staging process produces smaller, easier-to-review commits and makes later troubleshooting, reverting, and code review significantly safer."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-2-topic-4-section-3",
        "title": "Commit Metadata, Author Identity, and Timestamps",
        "content": "A Git commit records author and committer metadata. The author identifies the person who originally wrote the change, while the committer identifies the person or process that created the commit object. These values can differ when a patch is applied by another developer, a commit is cherry-picked, or history is rewritten. Each identity normally includes a name and email address. Git also records timestamps and timezone information for author and committer dates.\n\nConfigure identity with `git config user.name \"Developer Name\"` and `git config user.email \"developer@example.com\"` for a repository-specific identity. Use `git config --global` when the same identity should apply to repositories by default. Inspect effective values with `git config user.name` and `git config user.email`. Repository-level configuration can override global configuration, so check the repository settings when commits show an unexpected identity.\n\nCommit metadata is useful for attribution, auditing, and understanding how changes were produced, but it is not cryptographic proof of identity by itself. Signed commits can provide stronger verification when the organization configures trusted signing keys and verification policies. Do not place passwords, tokens, customer information, or confidential incident details in commit messages or metadata. Keep identity configuration accurate, especially when using separate personal and corporate repositories."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-2-topic-4-section-4",
        "title": "Commit Message Standards and Engineering Communication",
        "content": "A commit message explains why a change was made and gives future developers a useful description of the recorded work. A good subject line is concise, action-oriented, and specific. For example, `Fix retry handling for payment requests` communicates more than `Changes` or `Update code`. The message should describe the actual change rather than making unsupported claims about its quality or impact.\n\nA common convention uses a short subject followed by an optional blank line and a detailed body. The body can explain the problem, the chosen solution, relevant trade-offs, and any operational implications. Teams may adopt Conventional Commits, using prefixes such as feat, fix, docs, refactor, test, and chore. For example, `fix(api): handle expired access tokens` can support automated changelog generation and release classification when the project configures those tools.\n\nReference issue identifiers where useful, but do not make a ticket number the entire explanation. Avoid vague messages, misleading descriptions, and sensitive information. A commit message should remain understandable when viewed months later without requiring the reader to reconstruct the original conversation. Consistent messages improve repository search, release notes, incident investigation, and collaboration across development and operations teams."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-2-topic-4-section-5",
        "title": "Inspecting Commit History with Git Log",
        "content": "The `git log` command displays commit history and supports multiple formats for different investigation needs. Run `git log` for the full history, `git log --oneline` for a compact summary, and `git log --graph --oneline --decorate --all` to visualize branch relationships. The `--all` option includes reachable history from all references, while `--decorate` displays branch and tag names associated with commits.\n\nUse `git log -5` to inspect the most recent five commits, `git log --author=\"Developer Name\"` to filter by author, and `git log --since=\"2026-01-01\"` to limit results by date. Search commit messages with `git log --grep=\"timeout\"`. Use `git log -- path/to/file` to inspect commits affecting a particular path. Combine filters carefully because different options can change how Git selects and displays commits.\n\nFor a production investigation, identify the deployed commit first, then inspect its ancestors and related changes. Compare a known-good revision with the problematic revision using `git log BASE..HEAD` or `git diff BASE..HEAD`. Remember that history commands display commits reachable from selected references; a commit that is no longer reachable from a branch or tag may not appear in ordinary history views. Use reflogs and object recovery techniques when investigating recently moved references."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-2-topic-4-section-6",
        "title": "Inspecting Commit Changes, File History, and Patch Details",
        "content": "A commit's metadata explains who created it and when, but understanding the actual code change requires inspecting its patch. Use `git show HEAD` to display the latest commit and its changes. Use `git show --stat HEAD` for a summary of changed files and line counts, and `git show --name-only HEAD` to list affected paths. A specific commit can be inspected with `git show <commit-id>`.\n\nUse `git diff <older-commit> <newer-commit>` to compare two revisions. Use `git diff HEAD~1 HEAD` to compare the latest commit with its first parent when the revision relationship is appropriate. For file-specific history, use `git log --follow -- path/to/file` to continue history across many file renames, and `git blame path/to/file` to inspect the commit associated with individual lines.\n\nGit blame is an investigation aid, not a reliable way to assign fault. A line may have been moved, reformatted, or copied, and the displayed commit may not represent the original design decision. Use `git blame -w` to ignore whitespace changes where useful, and inspect the referenced commit and its surrounding context. Review complete patches before drawing conclusions about regressions, security defects, or production behavior."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-2-topic-4-section-7",
        "title": "Commit Graph, Parent Relationships, and Revision References",
        "content": "Git history is a directed graph of commits connected through parent references. A normal commit has one parent, while a merge commit generally has two or more parents. Branch names are movable references pointing to commits, and tags can provide named references to particular revisions. Understanding this graph is essential when comparing branches, tracing merged changes, and deciding which commits are included in a release.\n\nRevision expressions make it possible to navigate history. `HEAD` identifies the currently checked-out commit, `HEAD~1` identifies its first-parent predecessor, and `HEAD~3` moves three first-parent steps backward. `HEAD^` identifies the first parent, while `HEAD^2` identifies the second parent of a merge commit. These expressions are not interchangeable in every graph because a merge commit has multiple parent relationships.\n\nCommands such as `git log --graph --oneline --all` and `git show --pretty=raw <commit-id>` help reveal the graph and parent information. Use `git merge-base branch-a branch-b` to find a common ancestor when investigating divergence. A correct understanding of ancestry prevents mistakes when reverting merges, comparing release branches, or deciding whether a change has already been integrated."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-2-topic-4-section-8",
        "title": "Amending Commits, Resetting Changes, and Safe History Editing",
        "content": "Git provides commands to correct local commit history, but the safety of an operation depends on whether the affected commits have already been shared. `git commit --amend` replaces the latest commit with a new commit, allowing a developer to correct its message or include additional staged changes. The original commit object is replaced by a different commit identifier because the commit content or metadata changes.\n\n`git reset` moves the current branch reference and can also update the staging area or working tree depending on the selected mode. `git reset --soft HEAD~1` moves the branch reference backward while keeping changes staged. `git reset --mixed HEAD~1` keeps changes in the working tree but removes them from the staging area. `git reset --hard HEAD~1` also resets tracked working-tree content and can discard local modifications. Use destructive reset operations only after checking repository state and preserving any work that must remain.\n\nDo not rewrite shared branch history casually. Other developers may have based work on the original commits, and replacing them can require coordination and recovery. Prefer `git revert <commit-id>` when a change has already been shared and needs to be undone safely. Before amending or resetting, inspect `git status`, create a temporary branch or backup reference if necessary, and confirm which commits are local versus published."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-2-topic-4-section-9",
        "title": "Revert, Cherry-Pick, and Commit-Level Change Management",
        "content": "`git revert` creates a new commit that reverses the changes introduced by an earlier commit. It is generally appropriate for undoing changes that have already been shared because it preserves the existing history. For example, `git revert <commit-id>` creates a new reversal commit, subject to conflict resolution if later changes depend on the original modification. Reverting a merge commit requires choosing the appropriate mainline parent with the `-m` option.\n\n`git cherry-pick <commit-id>` applies the change introduced by an existing commit onto the current branch and creates a new commit. This is useful when a specific fix must be transferred to a maintenance branch without merging an entire development branch. Cherry-picking creates a new commit identity, even when the resulting patch is equivalent to the original change.\n\nBefore reverting or cherry-picking, inspect the target commit, its parent relationships, and the destination branch. Run relevant tests after applying the change and record why the operation was necessary. Avoid repeatedly cherry-picking the same logical change without understanding its history, because duplicated patches can create confusion during later merges. In release maintenance, track the original commit and every propagated copy so that fixes remain traceable across supported versions."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-2-topic-4-section-10",
        "title": "Finding Regressions with Git Bisect and Commit Search",
        "content": "Git bisect performs a binary search through commit history to identify the change that introduced a regression. Start with `git bisect start`, mark the current problematic revision with `git bisect bad`, and mark a known-good revision with `git bisect good <commit-id>`. Git checks out an intermediate commit. Test that revision and mark it good or bad. Git then narrows the remaining range until it identifies a likely first bad commit.\n\nThe investigation is most effective when the regression can be reproduced consistently. Define a repeatable test before starting, such as a unit test, integration test, command-line check, or controlled reproduction procedure. When possible, automate the check using `git bisect run <test-command>`. The test command must return an appropriate exit status and should not depend on uncontrolled external services or changing production data.\n\nFinish the operation with `git bisect reset` to return to the original branch or state. Inspect the suspected commit with `git show`, review related changes, and confirm the finding with a direct reproduction. Bisect identifies a commit boundary based on the test result; it does not independently prove root cause. If tests are flaky, commits are not buildable, or the regression depends on external state, use additional evidence and manual investigation."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-2-topic-4-section-11",
        "title": "Commit Signing, Verification, and Repository Auditability",
        "content": "Commit signing adds a cryptographic signature to commit metadata so that a configured verification process can check whether the commit was signed by a key associated with an expected identity. Git supports signing mechanisms such as GPG and SSH signing, depending on the installed Git version and configuration. Signing can be enabled through Git configuration, for example with `git config commit.gpgsign true`, after the signing key and verification environment have been configured correctly.\n\nA signature is useful only when the organization manages trusted keys, verifies signatures, and defines how identities are associated with developers or automation systems. A signed commit should not automatically be considered secure or correct: signing does not prove that the code passed tests, contains no vulnerabilities, or was reviewed appropriately. Those requirements need separate controls such as protected branches, CI checks, access restrictions, and security scanning.\n\nFor auditability, retain commit identifiers, signatures where required, pull request references, review decisions, build records, and deployment metadata. Validate signing configuration in a test repository before enforcing it across a production repository. Document key rotation, revoked keys, developer onboarding, and automated commit signing. These controls make commit provenance more dependable without confusing authenticity verification with software quality assurance."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-2-topic-4-section-12",
        "title": "Production Commit Investigation, Recovery, and Team Exercise",
        "content": "In production troubleshooting, commit history helps establish exactly which source change was included in a deployed release. Begin by identifying the deployment's commit identifier from the release record, build metadata, or deployment system. Confirm the corresponding tag and branch, inspect the commit with `git show`, and compare it with the last known-good revision. Review the pull request, test results, dependency changes, and configuration modifications associated with the suspected change.\n\nIf a recent commit caused a production regression, determine whether the safest response is a revert, a corrective commit, or a controlled rollback to a previously verified artifact. A revert is often suitable for shared history, while rebuilding an older source revision may not reproduce the exact artifact previously deployed if dependencies or build environments have changed. Prefer redeploying an immutable, previously verified artifact when the release process supports it. Preserve incident evidence and record the decision, approval, and validation results.\n\nPractical exercise: create a test repository, make several focused commits, create a feature branch, merge it, and inspect the resulting graph. Amend an unpublished commit, revert a published commit, cherry-pick a fix to a maintenance branch, and use bisect to locate an intentionally introduced test failure. Finally, inspect author metadata, patch contents, and commit signatures if configured. Confirm that every operation has a clear purpose and that recovery steps do not destroy unrelated work."
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "topicTitle": "Pull Request Lifecycle",
    "sections": [
      {
        "id": "stage-2-topic-5-section-1",
        "title": "Pull Request Fundamentals and Collaboration Model",
        "content": "A pull request is a proposal to integrate changes from one branch into another branch. It provides a structured collaboration point where developers can explain their work, discuss implementation decisions, review code, run automated checks, and obtain the approvals required by repository policy. On GitHub, a pull request compares a head branch containing proposed changes with a base branch that will receive those changes. The pull request itself is a collaboration record; it does not automatically mean that the changes are safe or ready for production.\n\nA typical pull request begins after a developer creates a focused branch, commits changes, pushes the branch to the remote repository, and opens a request against the intended target branch. GitHub calculates the differences and displays changed files, commits, and conversation activity. Reviewers can comment on individual lines, request changes, approve the proposal, or ask for clarification. Automated workflows can validate the proposed changes before integration.\n\nPull requests improve traceability because discussions, approvals, status checks, and merge information remain associated with the proposed change. They should be small enough to review carefully and should explain the business or technical reason for the modification. A pull request is not a substitute for testing, security controls, or deployment validation; it is one part of a controlled software delivery process."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-2-topic-5-section-2",
        "title": "Source Branch, Target Branch, and Comparison Direction",
        "content": "Every pull request has a head branch and a base branch. The head branch contains the proposed changes, while the base branch is the destination into which those changes are intended to be integrated. For example, a developer may propose merging feature/customer-search into main. In that case, feature/customer-search is the head branch and main is the base branch. Selecting the wrong base can introduce changes into an unintended release or maintenance line.\n\nBefore creating a pull request, update the local references and confirm the intended branch relationship. Useful commands include `git fetch origin`, `git branch --show-current`, `git status`, and `git log --oneline --decorate --graph --all`. Compare the source branch with its intended target using `git diff origin/main...HEAD` when working from a feature branch based on main. The three-dot comparison helps show changes introduced on the source side since the common ancestor.\n\nRepositories with develop, release, and maintenance branches need explicit target-branch rules. A feature intended for the next release should not accidentally target an older production maintenance branch. Check the pull request's base branch before requesting review and again before merging, especially when the target branch changes during release preparation."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-2-topic-5-section-3",
        "title": "Preparing a Focused Pull Request and Managing Scope",
        "content": "A high-quality pull request contains one coherent change with a clear purpose. Before opening it, inspect the working tree, staged changes, commit history, and final diff. Remove unrelated formatting changes, temporary files, generated artifacts, debugging statements, local environment files, and accidental credentials. Run relevant local tests and confirm that the branch is based on the correct target branch.\n\nA focused change is easier to understand and safer to review. If a task includes an API change, a database migration, and an unrelated documentation update, consider whether these belong together or should be separated into independently reviewable changes. Some changes must remain together because they form one atomic feature or migration; use engineering judgment rather than splitting mechanically.\n\nCheck that the branch contains the intended commits and that the pull request does not include unrelated commits from another developer. If the branch has drifted, synchronize it using the team's merge or rebase policy. The final diff should match the stated purpose. A reviewer should be able to understand what changed, why it changed, how it was tested, and what operational risks need attention without reconstructing the entire development history."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-2-topic-5-section-4",
        "title": "Writing Effective Pull Request Titles and Descriptions",
        "content": "A pull request title should communicate the change in a concise and searchable way. Titles such as `Add pagination to customer search API` or `Fix timeout handling in payment worker` are more useful than `Changes`, `Final update`, or `Bug fix`. Consistent title conventions can support release reporting and automation, especially when the repository uses Conventional Commits or issue-linked development practices.\n\nThe description should explain the problem or requirement, the solution, important implementation decisions, and the validation performed. Include relevant issue references, API or database implications, configuration changes, migration requirements, feature-flag behavior, and deployment considerations. A useful testing section states which commands or automated checks were run and identifies any limitations. Do not claim that tests passed unless they were actually executed.\n\nFor changes with operational impact, describe monitoring expectations, rollback considerations, compatibility requirements, and any manual action needed after deployment. Link relevant design documents or incident records when appropriate. Avoid copying large amounts of source code into the description when the diff already provides it. The description should help reviewers make an informed decision and provide future maintainers with context after the original discussion has ended."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-2-topic-5-section-5",
        "title": "Draft Pull Requests, Early Feedback, and Work-in-Progress Reviews",
        "content": "A draft pull request allows a developer to share work before it is ready for final approval. It is useful when an implementation needs early architectural feedback, when multiple developers are collaborating, or when a change is still incomplete. On GitHub, a draft pull request clearly indicates that the proposal is not yet ready to merge and prevents the normal ready-for-review workflow until the author converts it.\n\nEarly feedback can reveal incorrect assumptions before a large implementation is complete. A developer can share the intended design, known limitations, open questions, and planned validation. Reviewers can discuss API compatibility, data model choices, performance, security, and operational implications while changes are still inexpensive to adjust.\n\nA draft should not be used to bypass required checks or to treat unfinished code as production-ready. Keep the description updated as implementation decisions change. Before marking it ready, complete the agreed scope, run the relevant tests, remove temporary code, confirm the target branch, and ensure the diff is understandable. Convert the draft to ready for review only when the author is explicitly requesting a formal review and the repository's readiness expectations have been met."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-2-topic-5-section-6",
        "title": "Automated Pull Request Checks and Continuous Integration",
        "content": "Automated checks provide repeatable evidence about a proposed change. A pull request workflow may run formatting checks, linting, compilation, unit tests, integration tests, dependency analysis, secret detection, container image scanning, infrastructure validation, and policy checks. The appropriate checks depend on the repository's technology and risk profile. Fast checks should generally provide early feedback, while expensive suites can run in parallel or at later validation stages.\n\nGitHub Actions workflows can be triggered by pull request events such as opened, synchronized, reopened, or ready-for-review. A workflow can use the pull request's head commit to build and test the proposed change. Required status checks can then prevent merging until designated jobs succeed. Ensure that the workflow tests the intended source revision and that the result is associated with the correct pull request and commit.\n\nA green status is meaningful only when the check is correctly configured and trustworthy. Review workflows for overly broad permissions, untrusted code execution, unsafe use of secrets, and third-party action supply-chain risks. Avoid exposing privileged deployment credentials to untrusted pull request code, especially from forks. Failed checks should provide actionable logs and a clear owner for investigation rather than being routinely bypassed."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-2-topic-5-section-7",
        "title": "Reviewer Assignment, Ownership, and Review Readiness",
        "content": "Reviewer assignment should reflect the code ownership, technical expertise, and risk associated with the change. A reviewer may be selected manually, through a CODEOWNERS file, or by repository automation. CODEOWNERS can identify responsible teams or individuals for particular paths, such as application code, infrastructure definitions, security configuration, or deployment workflows. Required review policies can then ensure that designated owners participate before integration.\n\nThe pull request author should request review only after providing sufficient context and a reviewable diff. Reviewers need to know the purpose of the change, the expected behavior, important design decisions, and which areas deserve special attention. Large changes may benefit from an initial design review followed by a focused implementation review. Avoid assigning every team member to every request because excessive notifications can reduce review quality.\n\nReview ownership should be explicit for high-risk areas such as authentication, authorization, payment processing, database migrations, production infrastructure, and secret-handling workflows. A reviewer should decline or request additional expertise when the change is outside their competence. Approval should represent an informed review, not merely an administrative click. Teams should define escalation paths when required reviewers are unavailable."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-2-topic-5-section-8",
        "title": "Pull Request Review States, Comments, and Change Requests",
        "content": "Reviewers typically provide feedback through comments on specific lines or through an overall review decision. A comment can ask a question, explain a concern, suggest an improvement, or identify a defect. An approval indicates that the reviewer accepts the change under the applicable review policy. A request-changes decision indicates that modifications are required before the reviewer considers the proposal ready. The exact enforcement behavior depends on repository settings and branch protection rules.\n\nGood review comments identify the relevant behavior and explain why it matters. For example, a reviewer may point out that an API retry loop can duplicate a non-idempotent operation, or that a database query lacks an appropriate index. When possible, suggest a safe alternative or describe the expected outcome. Avoid personal criticism, vague statements, or comments that do not explain the technical concern.\n\nAuthors should respond to feedback respectfully, make corrections in focused commits, and explain when they disagree with a suggestion. Resolve conversations only when the concern has been addressed or an explicit decision has been recorded. A resolved comment should not be used to hide an unresolved production risk. Reviewers should recheck modified lines and the final diff before approving changes that previously required corrections."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-2-topic-5-section-9",
        "title": "Updating Pull Requests and Handling New Commits",
        "content": "A pull request changes as its source branch receives new commits. Pushing additional commits updates the proposed diff and normally triggers applicable pull request workflows again. This allows authors to address review feedback without creating an entirely new request. However, new commits can also introduce unrelated changes or invalidate earlier reviewer conclusions, so the final diff must be reviewed again after significant updates.\n\nWhen the target branch advances, the pull request may become outdated or develop merge conflicts. Synchronize the feature branch according to the repository's merge or rebase policy. After resolving conflicts, inspect the resulting diff carefully because a technically successful conflict resolution can still produce incorrect application behavior. Re-run relevant tests and confirm that the pull request's checks correspond to the latest source commit.\n\nIf the pull request's base branch changes, review the newly calculated diff and verify that the destination is correct. Avoid force-pushing shared branches without coordination. If history must be rewritten on a personal branch, communicate with reviewers and use safer force-push practices such as `--force-with-lease`. Keep the discussion and final implementation aligned so reviewers are not approving a version that has since changed materially."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-2-topic-5-section-10",
        "title": "Merge Readiness, Merge Queue, and Final Integration",
        "content": "Before merging, confirm that the pull request targets the correct branch, the final diff matches the intended scope, required approvals are present, required status checks have passed, and no unresolved blocking discussions remain. Also verify that the source branch has not changed since the final review in a way that invalidates the approval. Repository rules may enforce these conditions automatically, but authors and maintainers should still understand the merge requirements.\n\nA merge queue can coordinate integration when many pull requests target the same busy branch. It validates proposed changes against the latest target-branch state and may test combinations of queued changes before integrating them. This reduces the risk that individually passing pull requests break the shared branch when merged together. The exact behavior depends on the platform and repository configuration.\n\nOnce merge requirements are satisfied, use the merge method selected by repository policy. Different strategies preserve or transform commit history in different ways. Confirm that the resulting target branch contains the intended change and that downstream CI/CD workflows execute as expected. For critical changes, verify the resulting artifact and deployment record rather than treating the merge event itself as proof of successful production delivery."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-2-topic-5-section-11",
        "title": "Pull Request Closure, Reopening, and Abandonment",
        "content": "A pull request can be merged, closed without merging, or reopened when additional work is required. A merged pull request records that its changes were integrated according to the selected merge method. A closed pull request may represent an abandoned implementation, a duplicate proposal, a change superseded by another approach, or work that is no longer required. The closure reason should be understandable from the conversation or linked issue.\n\nBefore closing a pull request without merging, confirm whether its work contains commits that need to be retained elsewhere. The source branch may still contain useful changes, and deleting it immediately could complicate recovery. If the request is superseded, link the replacement pull request or issue. If it is blocked, record the reason and the next action rather than leaving contributors uncertain.\n\nReopening a pull request should be accompanied by a check of its source branch, base branch, current diff, approvals, and status checks. Earlier approvals may no longer be valid if the code or target branch has changed. Branch cleanup should follow the repository's retention policy and should not remove the only recoverable reference to unmerged work. Maintain a clear lifecycle so that open, merged, and abandoned proposals remain easy to understand."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-2-topic-5-section-12",
        "title": "Production Pull Request Workflow, Audit Trail, and Practical Exercise",
        "content": "In a production engineering workflow, a pull request should connect a requirement to a reviewed implementation and a traceable delivery artifact. A practical sequence is to create a focused branch, implement the change, run local validation, push the branch, and open a pull request against the correct target. The author documents the purpose, testing, compatibility, and operational impact. Reviewers examine the implementation, automated workflows validate it, and repository rules enforce required approvals and checks before merge.\n\nFor a high-risk change, include additional validation such as security review, database migration testing, backward-compatibility checks, performance evaluation, or infrastructure plan review. After merge, verify that the expected CI/CD pipeline ran against the merged commit, produced the intended artifact, and recorded its source revision. Deployment approval and production verification remain separate controls. A merged pull request is not equivalent to a successful production deployment.\n\nPractical exercise: create a feature branch, implement a small change, open a draft pull request, request early feedback, and then mark it ready for review. Configure an automated test workflow, add a reviewer, address a requested change, push an update, and inspect the refreshed checks. Merge only after all required conditions pass. Finally, trace the merged commit to its build and deployment record. Document how the process would change for an emergency hotfix or a regulated production release."
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "topicTitle": "Code Review",
    "sections": [
      {
        "id": "stage-2-topic-6-section-1",
        "title": "Code Review Principles, Objectives, and Engineering Responsibility",
        "content": "Code review is a systematic examination of proposed source-code changes by people other than the original author. Its purpose is to improve correctness, maintainability, security, reliability, and shared understanding of the codebase. A review should evaluate whether the implementation satisfies its requirement, fits the existing architecture, handles expected and failure conditions, and can be safely maintained by the team. It is not simply a search for formatting mistakes or a process for approving another developer's work without analysis.\n\nReviewers should examine behavior rather than personal coding preferences. A technically correct change may still introduce performance problems, unsafe data handling, backward incompatibility, or operational complexity. Reviewers should ask whether the code behaves correctly under realistic inputs, whether errors are handled safely, and whether the implementation can be tested and observed in production. They should also consider whether the change introduces unnecessary complexity or duplicates existing functionality.\n\nCode review is a shared engineering responsibility. Authors should make changes understandable and provide useful context. Reviewers should be respectful, specific, and evidence-driven. Teams should establish review expectations based on risk and system criticality, while avoiding unnecessary delays for low-risk changes. The goal is to improve the software and transfer technical knowledge, not to establish personal authority over another developer."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-2-topic-6-section-2",
        "title": "Review Preparation, Diff Navigation, and Change Scope Analysis",
        "content": "A useful review begins with understanding the purpose and scope of the change. Read the issue or requirement, inspect the pull request description, identify affected modules, and understand the expected behavior before examining individual lines. Determine whether the change affects APIs, database schemas, configuration, deployment manifests, infrastructure, authentication, or other components with operational impact.\n\nStart with the overall diff and file list. Identify unexpectedly modified files, generated output, dependency changes, configuration changes, and large unrelated formatting updates. Then inspect the changes in logical groups. Useful Git commands include `git diff BASE...HEAD`, `git show --stat <commit-id>`, and `git diff --check`. On GitHub, use the Files changed view and review the complete patch rather than relying only on the summary.\n\nUnderstand the surrounding implementation before judging a changed line. A function may depend on validation performed by its caller, or a database transaction may be managed by a framework layer. Search for related call sites, tests, interfaces, and configuration. If the change is too large to understand reliably, ask the author to split it into smaller logical units or provide architectural context. Review quality depends on understanding the system, not merely reading the visible patch."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-2-topic-6-section-3",
        "title": "Correctness Review, Logic Validation, and Edge-Case Analysis",
        "content": "Correctness review determines whether the changed implementation produces the required behavior across normal, boundary, and failure conditions. Examine conditional logic, loops, data transformations, state changes, null handling, resource cleanup, and exception paths. Check whether the implementation handles empty collections, invalid inputs, duplicate requests, large values, missing records, and unexpected service responses where those conditions are relevant.\n\nTrace important execution paths from the entry point to the resulting state change. Verify assumptions about ordering, concurrency, retries, and transaction boundaries. A function that appears correct in isolation may fail when called multiple times or when two requests execute simultaneously. Check whether a retry can repeat a non-idempotent operation, whether an update can overwrite newer data, or whether an exception leaves a partially modified state.\n\nReviewers should distinguish confirmed defects from questions and optional improvements. When reporting a defect, explain the triggering condition, expected behavior, actual risk, and a possible correction. Ask for tests that reproduce important edge cases. Do not approve a change merely because the happy path works; production correctness depends on how the implementation behaves when assumptions are violated."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-2-topic-6-section-4",
        "title": "Security Code Review and Trust-Boundary Validation",
        "content": "Security review examines whether a change creates opportunities for unauthorized access, data exposure, injection, privilege escalation, or misuse of trusted components. Identify untrusted inputs such as HTTP parameters, request bodies, uploaded files, environment variables, message payloads, and external API responses. Verify that validation and output encoding are appropriate for the context. For database access, inspect whether queries use safe parameterization rather than unsafe string construction.\n\nReview authentication and authorization separately. Authentication establishes who or what is making a request; authorization determines which actions that identity is permitted to perform. Confirm that sensitive operations enforce authorization at the correct service or data boundary rather than relying only on user-interface restrictions. Check object-level access controls, tenant isolation, role checks, and administrative privileges where applicable.\n\nInspect secrets, tokens, certificates, and private configuration. Credentials should not be committed into source code, logs, test fixtures, or error messages. Check whether new dependencies or scripts execute untrusted content and whether workflows expose privileged credentials to untrusted pull request code. Security review should also consider unsafe deserialization, path traversal, server-side request forgery, insecure defaults, and overly broad permissions when relevant to the implementation. Escalate serious vulnerabilities instead of treating them as ordinary style comments."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-2-topic-6-section-5",
        "title": "Performance, Scalability, and Resource-Management Review",
        "content": "Performance review examines whether a change increases CPU usage, memory consumption, database load, network traffic, latency, or resource contention. Inspect loops, repeated computations, collection operations, serialization, caching, and external service calls. A small code change can create a major performance regression when executed for every request or across a large dataset.\n\nFor database changes, examine query patterns, indexes, pagination, transaction duration, locking, and the number of queries generated by the application. Watch for N+1 query patterns in which one initial query is followed by an additional query for every returned record. Check whether large result sets are loaded into memory unnecessarily and whether pagination or streaming is appropriate.\n\nReview connection pools, thread pools, file handles, sockets, and background workers. Ensure resources are released reliably and that timeouts and cancellation are handled. Caching should have a clear invalidation strategy and should not expose one user's data to another. Where performance impact is uncertain, request measurements, load-test results, query plans, or profiling evidence. Avoid speculative optimization, but do not ignore measurable risks introduced by a change."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-2-topic-6-section-6",
        "title": "Maintainability, Architecture, and Design Consistency",
        "content": "Maintainability review considers whether future developers can understand, modify, test, and extend the implementation without unnecessary risk. Examine naming, function responsibilities, module boundaries, dependency direction, duplication, error handling, and the separation of concerns. A method that performs validation, database access, network communication, business calculations, and logging may be difficult to test and maintain because it has too many responsibilities.\n\nCheck whether the change follows established architectural boundaries. For example, business rules should not become tightly coupled to HTTP controllers or infrastructure-specific clients when the project uses layered architecture. New dependencies should have a clear purpose and should not create circular references or unnecessary coupling. Reuse existing abstractions when they genuinely fit, but avoid introducing generic frameworks for a small isolated requirement.\n\nReadable code is not necessarily code with the fewest lines. Prefer clear control flow, meaningful names, and explicit behavior over clever expressions that hide important decisions. Comments should explain non-obvious reasoning rather than repeat what the code already says. Reviewers should recommend refactoring when it materially improves comprehension or reduces risk, while avoiding unrelated redesign requests that unnecessarily expand the change."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-2-topic-6-section-7",
        "title": "Test Quality Review, Coverage, and Regression Protection",
        "content": "Test review evaluates whether the implementation has appropriate evidence of correctness. Inspect unit tests for isolated business behavior, integration tests for interactions between components, and end-to-end tests for important user or system workflows. The appropriate test level depends on the change. A simple formatting adjustment may not require extensive integration tests, while an authorization change may need tests covering permitted and forbidden access paths.\n\nCheck that tests assert meaningful outcomes rather than merely executing code. Tests should cover normal behavior, boundary values, invalid input, expected failures, and relevant regression scenarios. A test that only verifies a method was called may miss an incorrect result. A test that depends on execution order, shared mutable state, or external services may become unreliable in CI.\n\nReview test data, cleanup, isolation, and environment assumptions. Ensure that tests do not contain production credentials or sensitive customer data. Check whether database migrations, API contracts, infrastructure changes, and backward compatibility need dedicated validation. Coverage percentages can identify untested areas, but high coverage alone does not prove that important behavior is correctly tested. Request focused tests that protect the actual risk introduced by the change."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-2-topic-6-section-8",
        "title": "API, Database, and Configuration Change Review",
        "content": "Changes to public APIs, database schemas, and application configuration deserve additional review because they can affect other services and existing deployments. For an API change, inspect request and response formats, status codes, validation rules, authentication requirements, pagination behavior, and compatibility with existing clients. Removing or renaming a field can break consumers even when the updated service compiles successfully.\n\nFor database changes, review migration ordering, schema compatibility, indexes, constraints, data transformations, locking, and rollback or forward-recovery procedures. Consider how the migration behaves while old and new application versions may run simultaneously during a rolling deployment. Expand-and-contract techniques can reduce risk by adding compatible structures first, migrating usage, and removing obsolete structures only after consumers have moved.\n\nFor configuration changes, inspect defaults, environment-specific values, validation, secret references, and behavior when a value is missing or malformed. Ensure that sensitive settings are not exposed through logs or committed files. A configuration option should have a clear purpose and documented operational behavior. Reviewers should ask how the change behaves in development, staging, and production, not just on a developer's local machine."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-2-topic-6-section-9",
        "title": "Review Feedback, Inline Comments, and Technical Discussion",
        "content": "Effective review feedback is specific, respectful, and connected to observable behavior. An inline comment should identify the relevant code and explain the concern. For example, instead of writing `This is wrong`, explain that a retry can submit the same payment twice when the remote service times out after processing the first request. A useful comment helps the author understand both the defect and its consequences.\n\nSeparate blocking issues from optional suggestions. A confirmed security vulnerability, data-loss risk, or correctness defect should be clearly identified as requiring correction. A naming preference or alternative implementation may be presented as a non-blocking suggestion. Teams can use consistent labels such as blocking, suggestion, question, and nit, provided their meaning is understood.\n\nAuthors should respond with evidence, code changes, or a technical explanation. Disagreement is acceptable when it is resolved through engineering reasoning. Avoid personal remarks, assumptions about competence, and repeated comments that do not add new information. When a concern is resolved, record the decision and close the conversation according to team practice. Serious unresolved issues should remain visible and must not disappear simply to make the review look complete."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-2-topic-6-section-10",
        "title": "Review Approval Standards, Risk Assessment, and Reviewer Accountability",
        "content": "Approval should represent an informed assessment of the change against the repository's engineering requirements. Reviewers should understand the affected behavior, examine the relevant diff, consider important risks, and verify that required checks and discussions have been addressed. An approval is not a guarantee that software is defect-free, but it is a recorded decision that the reviewer considers the change acceptable within the defined review process.\n\nReview depth should reflect risk. A documentation correction may need a lightweight review. A change to authentication, payment processing, production infrastructure, encryption, database migrations, or access-control policies may require specialist review and additional validation. Teams should define when security, platform, database, or service owners must participate. CODEOWNERS and required-review rules can help route sensitive changes to the appropriate people.\n\nReviewers should not approve changes they have not examined or do not understand. Authors should not pressure reviewers to approve merely to meet a deadline. If evidence is incomplete, request clarification or additional tests. Organizations should also define how emergency exceptions work, who can authorize them, how they are recorded, and how the normal review requirements are restored afterward."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-2-topic-6-section-11",
        "title": "Code Review Tooling, Static Analysis, and Automated Review Assistance",
        "content": "Review tooling helps teams identify issues consistently and reduce repetitive manual work. Static analysis tools inspect source code without executing the application and can detect suspicious patterns, common defects, complexity, and potential security problems. Linters enforce formatting and coding conventions. Dependency scanners identify known vulnerabilities in third-party packages, while secret scanners look for credentials accidentally added to source history or proposed changes.\n\nGitHub review interfaces support inline comments, suggestions, review decisions, CODEOWNERS routing, and integration with status checks. Automated bots can summarize changes, identify ownership, report test results, and highlight possible defects. These tools should provide evidence and suggestions rather than replace human understanding of business rules and system architecture.\n\nConfigure tools to minimize noisy findings and document how teams handle warnings, false positives, and accepted risks. A warning should not be ignored simply because it appears frequently; repeated noise may indicate poor rule configuration that needs correction. AI-assisted review can help locate suspicious patterns, but generated feedback must be independently verified. Do not send confidential source code or sensitive data to tools that are not approved for the organization's security and privacy requirements."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-2-topic-6-section-12",
        "title": "Production-Grade Code Review Workflow and Practical Review Exercise",
        "content": "A production-grade review combines technical inspection, risk assessment, test evidence, and clear decision-making. Begin by understanding the requirement and affected components. Review the complete diff, trace important execution paths, inspect security boundaries, assess performance and compatibility, and examine tests. Confirm that automated checks are meaningful and that the final code still matches the version under review. Escalate specialist concerns when the change affects sensitive systems or shared infrastructure.\n\nA practical review checklist should include correctness, input validation, authorization, error handling, resource management, logging, observability, test quality, dependency changes, configuration safety, backward compatibility, and rollback or recovery considerations. Not every item applies to every change, so reviewers should focus on relevant risks rather than mechanically checking boxes. Record important assumptions and unresolved limitations in the review discussion.\n\nExercise: review a sample API change that introduces a new endpoint, database query, and retry mechanism. Identify an authorization gap, an inefficient query, a missing boundary test, and a possible duplicate-operation risk. Write clear inline comments explaining each issue, request appropriate corrections, and verify the updated implementation. Finally, provide an approval decision with a short technical justification. The exercise should demonstrate that good review is evidence-based, constructive, and focused on production behavior."
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "topicTitle": "Branch Protection Rules",
    "sections": [
      {
        "id": "stage-2-topic-7-section-1",
        "title": "Branch Protection Fundamentals and Repository Governance",
        "content": "Branch protection is a repository governance mechanism that restricts unsafe or unauthorized changes to important branches. It establishes conditions that must be satisfied before a branch can receive updates. Typical protected branches include main, develop, release branches, and maintenance branches. The purpose is to prevent accidental direct changes, enforce review and testing requirements, and preserve the integrity of code used to build and deploy software.\n\nWithout appropriate controls, a developer may accidentally push unreviewed code, overwrite another person's work, delete an important branch, or introduce a change that bypasses required validation. Protection rules provide technical enforcement for the team's development policy. They complement code review, automated testing, access management, and release governance rather than replacing them.\n\nA protection policy should be based on the branch's role and risk. A production branch may require independent approval, successful CI checks, and restricted push permissions. A temporary development branch may need fewer restrictions. GitHub supports classic branch protection rules and repository rulesets, depending on repository configuration and plan capabilities. Administrators should document the intended controls, test their behavior, and periodically verify that exceptions and bypass permissions remain justified."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-2-topic-7-section-2",
        "title": "Classic Branch Protection Rules and Target Branch Patterns",
        "content": "Classic GitHub branch protection rules apply selected restrictions to branches matching a configured branch name or pattern. A repository administrator can create a rule for main, release/*, or another pattern according to the repository's naming convention. The rule can require pull requests, approvals, status checks, signed commits, or other supported controls. It can also restrict force pushes and branch deletion.\n\nA rule for main protects the primary integration branch, while a pattern such as release/* can protect release branches created over time. Pattern selection must be deliberate. An overly broad pattern may affect temporary branches unexpectedly, while a narrow pattern may leave important branches unprotected. Check how patterns interact with actual branch names and whether multiple matching rules create ambiguous expectations.\n\nWhen configuring classic rules, verify the selected repository and branch pattern before saving. Test with a representative developer account rather than relying only on administrator access. Confirm that direct pushes, pull requests, force pushes, and deletions behave as intended. Keep the policy aligned with the team's branching strategy so that enforcement does not accidentally block legitimate workflows or leave a production path without protection."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-2-topic-7-section-3",
        "title": "GitHub Repository Rulesets and Layered Enforcement",
        "content": "GitHub repository rulesets provide a policy-based way to govern branches and, where supported, tags. A ruleset can target selected references and define restrictions or required conditions. Depending on repository capabilities, rulesets can be configured at repository or organization level, allowing administrators to apply consistent policies across multiple repositories.\n\nRulesets can help centralize controls for production branches, release references, and other important naming patterns. They support enforcement modes such as active enforcement and evaluation-oriented modes where available. Evaluation allows administrators to understand which changes would be blocked before enabling a rule for normal use. This is useful when introducing stricter controls into repositories with existing automation and contributor workflows.\n\nRulesets should be reviewed as part of an overall governance design. Determine which repositories and references are targeted, which roles can bypass enforcement, and how exceptions are recorded. Avoid assuming that a policy is effective merely because it exists in the settings interface. Test it using realistic identities and operations, inspect policy evaluation results where available, and confirm that organization-level and repository-level policies produce the intended combined behavior."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-2-topic-7-section-4",
        "title": "Pull Request Requirements and Independent Approval Controls",
        "content": "Requiring pull requests before changes reach a protected branch creates a controlled integration point. Instead of pushing directly to main, developers propose changes through a pull request where reviewers can inspect the diff, discuss implementation decisions, and verify automated checks. This supports traceability and makes it harder for unreviewed work to enter important branches.\n\nApproval requirements specify how many approving reviews are needed before a pull request can be merged. A production repository may require one or more independent approvals, depending on risk and organizational policy. Administrators should consider whether the pull request author can approve their own work, whether the most recent review must approve the latest changes, and whether code-owner approval is required for sensitive paths. Exact options depend on the GitHub rule configuration available to the repository.\n\nApproval count alone does not guarantee review quality. Assign reviewers with appropriate technical knowledge, use CODEOWNERS for important components, and require additional specialist review for sensitive changes. Test the policy with author, reviewer, maintainer, and administrator roles. Confirm that the intended approval conditions are actually enforced and that stale approvals are handled according to the repository's risk requirements."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-2-topic-7-section-5",
        "title": "Required Status Checks and CI Validation Enforcement",
        "content": "Required status checks prevent a protected branch from accepting a pull request until designated automated validations succeed. These checks may include compilation, unit tests, integration tests, linting, security scanning, dependency analysis, infrastructure validation, and policy checks. A repository should select checks that provide meaningful evidence for the type of change being merged.\n\nA status check is associated with a commit or pull request validation result. When configuring required checks, select the correct check names and, where supported, the expected GitHub Actions integration or application source. Avoid ambiguous or duplicate check names because an unrelated workflow could produce a similarly named result. Ensure that the workflow runs for the intended pull request events and reports a final status even when a job is skipped or fails.\n\nA common operational problem occurs when a required check is renamed or removed from the CI workflow. Pull requests may then remain blocked because GitHub is waiting for a check that no longer reports a result. Update branch rules and workflow definitions together. Test the configuration using a sample pull request and verify that failed checks block integration while successful checks permit the next required policy conditions to be evaluated."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-2-topic-7-section-6",
        "title": "CODEOWNERS, Required Reviewers, and Sensitive Path Governance",
        "content": "CODEOWNERS defines which users or teams are responsible for reviewing changes to particular repository paths. A CODEOWNERS file can be stored in supported repository locations and uses path patterns to map files to GitHub usernames or teams. For example, application source, infrastructure manifests, database migrations, and security workflows can have different owners.\n\nCode ownership helps route reviews to people who understand the affected components. When combined with branch protection settings that require code-owner review, it can enforce specialist participation for sensitive changes. However, a CODEOWNERS file does not independently guarantee that every assigned reviewer will approve a change. The repository must enable the relevant review requirement, and the assigned teams must have appropriate repository access.\n\nReview the file for correct paths, valid team references, and rule ordering. Later matching patterns can affect ownership for particular paths, so test representative files rather than assuming the mapping is obvious. Maintain ownership as teams and repository structure change. Avoid assigning a single unavailable individual as the only owner for critical production code; use an appropriate team with a documented escalation process."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-2-topic-7-section-7",
        "title": "Force Push Restrictions, Branch Deletion Controls, and History Integrity",
        "content": "Force pushing replaces the remote branch reference with a different commit history. It can remove commits from the visible branch history and disrupt other developers who based their work on the previous version. On important shared branches, unrestricted force pushes create a serious risk of accidental history loss. Protection policies can block force pushes so that normal collaboration preserves expected branch history.\n\nBranch deletion controls prevent important references from being removed accidentally or without authorization. Deleting main, an active release branch, or a maintenance branch can disrupt builds, deployments, and recovery procedures. Protect production and release references according to their operational importance. Temporary feature branches may follow a less restrictive cleanup policy after successful integration.\n\nForce-push and deletion restrictions should be tested using both normal contributor and authorized administrator accounts. Understand how bypass permissions interact with these restrictions, because a person who can bypass a rule may still perform operations that ordinary contributors cannot. For recovery, use known commit IDs, tags, remote references, and reflogs where available. Never assume a deleted branch can always be restored without retained references."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-2-topic-7-section-8",
        "title": "Signed Commit Requirements and Verified Source Provenance",
        "content": "Signed commits use cryptographic signing to provide evidence that a commit was created with a signing key associated with a configured identity. Git supports signing mechanisms such as GPG and SSH, depending on the environment and configuration. GitHub can display signature verification information for supported signing configurations. Repository policy may require signed commits on selected protected branches.\n\nA signing requirement can improve provenance controls, especially in repositories with strict compliance or software supply-chain requirements. Developers must configure their signing keys correctly, protect private keys, and ensure that their identity information matches the verification configuration. Automated systems that create commits must also be configured to sign them when policy requires it.\n\nA verified signature does not prove that code is correct, safe, or reviewed. It proves a narrower property about the signed commit and the associated verification process. Combine signature requirements with code review, required status checks, access controls, dependency scanning, and artifact provenance. Before enforcing the policy, test local developer workflows, bots, merge methods, and automated release processes to prevent unexpected integration failures."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-2-topic-7-section-9",
        "title": "Administrator Bypass, Repository Roles, and Exception Governance",
        "content": "Some repository protection configurations allow administrators or selected roles to bypass particular restrictions. Bypass capability can be necessary for controlled recovery, emergency changes, or policy administration, but unrestricted use weakens the protection model. A production repository should identify which roles can bypass rules, which operations are permitted, and what evidence must be recorded when an exception is used.\n\nApply least privilege. Developers who need to contribute code should not automatically receive permission to change repository governance or bypass production controls. Separate repository administration, development, release approval, and deployment responsibilities where the organization's risk model requires it. Review organization teams and repository roles regularly, especially after team changes or employee departures.\n\nEmergency exceptions should have a defined approval path, a documented reason, an identifiable actor, and a follow-up review. Restore normal enforcement immediately after the exception. Examine repository audit events and available activity records to identify unexpected bypass behavior. Do not use administrator access as a routine shortcut for blocked pull requests; investigate why the normal validation or approval process failed."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-2-topic-7-section-10",
        "title": "Protection Rules for Release Branches, Tags, and Deployment References",
        "content": "Release branches and tags identify code intended for a particular release or maintained production version. These references may be used by build systems, deployment pipelines, support teams, and incident responders. Accidental modification or deletion can make it difficult to reproduce a release or identify the source of a deployed artifact.\n\nApply branch rules to release naming patterns such as release/* when those branches are part of the repository workflow. Where supported, use tag protection or rulesets to restrict who can create, update, or delete release tags. A release tag should identify a deliberate commit and should follow the project's versioning policy. Avoid moving an existing production tag to a different commit without a documented correction process.\n\nThe pipeline should record the exact source commit, tag, build identifier, and artifact digest. Protection of the Git reference is only one part of release integrity; artifact storage, signing, deployment authorization, and environment access must also be controlled. Test how release automation interacts with protected references, particularly when automated tools create tags or update release branches."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-2-topic-7-section-11",
        "title": "Testing, Auditing, and Troubleshooting Branch Protection Policies",
        "content": "Branch protection policies should be validated through actual repository operations. Create a test branch or use a controlled test repository, then attempt direct pushes, pull requests without approvals, merges with failing checks, force pushes, and branch deletion. Repeat the tests using different roles so that the result reflects actual permissions rather than only administrator behavior.\n\nCommon failures include a required status check that no longer exists, an approval requirement that cannot be satisfied, a CODEOWNERS entry that references an inaccessible team, or a rule pattern that does not match the intended branch. Another frequent issue is a workflow that does not run for the pull request event expected by the branch policy. Diagnose the exact blocked condition before changing the protection rule.\n\nReview audit events and repository settings after policy changes. Record the rule owner, intended target branches, enforcement conditions, bypass roles, and last review date. For a failed merge, inspect the pull request's checks, review decisions, base branch, and applicable rules. Fix the underlying workflow or permission problem instead of disabling important controls simply to complete a merge."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-2-topic-7-section-12",
        "title": "Production Branch Governance, Policy Design, and Practical Exercise",
        "content": "A production branch governance design should define which branches are protected, which changes require pull requests, how many approvals are needed, which automated checks must pass, and who can bypass enforcement. It should also cover force-push restrictions, deletion controls, signed commits where required, code ownership, and tag governance. Policies must reflect the repository's branching model and the criticality of the software.\n\nPractical exercise: configure a test repository with a protected main branch. Require pull requests, independent approval, and a successful CI status check. Restrict force pushes and deletion. Add CODEOWNERS entries for application and infrastructure directories, then test a pull request that modifies each path. Verify that the correct owners are requested and that the required approvals are enforced.\n\nNext, deliberately rename a required CI check and observe how the merge becomes blocked. Correct the policy and verify the workflow again. Test normal contributor permissions and administrator bypass behavior. Finally, document the rules, exception process, audit evidence, and recovery approach. The result should be a policy that prevents unsafe integration without unnecessarily blocking legitimate engineering work."
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "topicTitle": "Merge Strategies",
    "sections": [
      {
        "id": "stage-2-topic-8-section-1",
        "title": "Git Merge Fundamentals and Integration Objectives",
        "content": "Git merge integrates changes from one branch into another. It is commonly used to bring completed feature work into a shared development or production branch. A merge combines the histories of the participating branches and records how their changes were integrated. The resulting history may contain a merge commit or may be represented by a fast-forward update, depending on the branch relationship and the selected merge strategy.\n\nFor example, a developer creates feature/payment-validation from main, makes several commits, and later integrates the feature into main. Git compares the branch histories and determines how the changes can be combined. If main has not advanced since the feature branch was created, a fast-forward may be possible. If both branches contain new commits, Git generally needs to create a merge commit or use another integration method.\n\nMerge strategy selection affects history readability, traceability, rollback, and collaboration. Teams should choose a policy that fits their branching model instead of selecting a method only because it produces a shorter log. Before merging, inspect the source branch, target branch, pending changes, test results, and repository protection requirements."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-2-topic-8-section-2",
        "title": "Fast-Forward Merge and Linear Branch Advancement",
        "content": "A fast-forward merge moves the target branch pointer forward when the target branch is an ancestor of the source branch. No merge commit is necessary because the target history can advance directly to the source branch's latest commit. The commit graph remains linear, which makes the sequence of commits easy to follow.\n\nFor example, if main points to commit A and feature/search contains commits B and C on top of A, merging feature/search into main can simply move main to C. The command `git merge --ff-only feature/search` requests this behavior and refuses to merge if a fast-forward is not possible. This is useful when a team wants linear history and prefers to handle divergence explicitly.\n\nFast-forward merging does not preserve a separate merge boundary showing when the feature was integrated. If main has advanced independently, the operation cannot fast-forward. The developer must first update the feature branch or choose another approved integration method. Use fast-forward only when the repository's history policy and review workflow support it. A successful fast-forward does not replace pull request approval or required CI validation."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-2-topic-8-section-3",
        "title": "Three-Way Merge and Merge Commit Creation",
        "content": "A three-way merge combines two branch tips using their common ancestor, also called the merge base. Git examines the changes made on both sides since that common point and attempts to produce a combined result. When the target branch and source branch have both advanced, a three-way merge can preserve both lines of development in the resulting history.\n\nWhen a merge commit is created, it normally has two parents: the previous target branch tip and the source branch tip. This records the integration event and makes the relationship between the branches visible in the commit graph. A team can use `git merge --no-ff feature/search` to create a merge commit even when a fast-forward would otherwise be possible.\n\nMerge commits are useful when teams want to preserve feature integration boundaries, especially in long-lived feature branches or workflows where the merge event has operational significance. However, excessive merge commits can make history harder to scan. Teams should agree whether to retain these boundaries, use pull request merge commits, or prefer a linear history policy."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-2-topic-8-section-4",
        "title": "Squash Merge and Consolidating Feature Changes",
        "content": "A squash merge combines the changes from a source branch into a single new commit on the target branch. Instead of preserving every source-branch commit as part of the target branch history, the integration records one consolidated commit representing the combined result. This can make the target branch history shorter and easier to read.\n\nSquash merging is useful when a feature branch contains many temporary commits such as work-in-progress updates, formatting fixes, or small corrections. A pull request with several commits can be merged as one meaningful commit with a clear message. GitHub provides a squash-and-merge option when the repository permits it, and Git also supports squash operations through commands such as `git merge --squash`.\n\nA squash merge does not preserve the original source commits as ancestors of the target branch. This distinction matters when developers continue working on the same source branch after the squash operation. Repeated integration can become confusing if the branch is not refreshed or recreated appropriately. Use squash merging when the team values a clean target history and does not need individual feature commits to remain part of the target branch ancestry."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-2-topic-8-section-5",
        "title": "Rebase-Based Integration and Commit History Rewriting",
        "content": "Rebase replays commits from one branch on top of another commit. It changes the base of the source branch and creates new commit IDs for the replayed commits because commit identity includes parent information. Rebase is commonly used to update a feature branch with the latest target branch changes before integration.\n\nFor example, a feature branch may have been created from an earlier main commit while main has received new work. Running `git fetch origin` followed by `git rebase origin/main` replays the feature commits on the latest main history. This can produce a linear sequence and expose conflicts before the pull request is merged.\n\nRebase is not the same as merging. It rewrites the source branch's commit ancestry rather than creating a merge commit that records both branch tips. Avoid rebasing commits that other developers already depend on unless the team has an agreed coordination process. For a branch already published and shared, use caution and communicate before any history rewrite. Rebase should not be used to bypass review or required validation."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-2-topic-8-section-6",
        "title": "GitHub Pull Request Merge Methods and Repository Configuration",
        "content": "GitHub pull requests can offer different merge methods according to repository settings. Common methods include creating a merge commit, squashing the pull request's commits into one commit, or rebasing the pull request commits onto the base branch. Repository administrators can enable or disable supported methods to enforce the team's integration policy.\n\nA merge commit preserves the relationship between the pull request branch and the target branch. Squash merging produces one target-branch commit for the pull request's combined changes. Rebase merging places the pull request commits onto the base branch without creating a merge commit. These methods create different commit graphs and affect how later branch synchronization and release investigation work.\n\nThe merge method should be selected deliberately. A team that requires visible feature boundaries may prefer merge commits. A team that wants concise history may choose squash merging. A team that wants linear history while preserving individual commits may prefer rebase merging. Confirm how the selected method interacts with required status checks, signed commit requirements, branch protection, and automation before changing repository settings."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-2-topic-8-section-7",
        "title": "Merge Base, Commit Graphs, and Ancestry Analysis",
        "content": "Understanding commit ancestry helps engineers predict the result of a merge. Git commits form a directed graph in which each commit points to its parent commit or commits. A normal commit usually has one parent, while a merge commit normally has multiple parents. The common ancestor of two branch tips provides the base from which Git compares their changes.\n\nThe command `git merge-base main feature/search` identifies a best common ancestor for the two references. `git log --graph --oneline --decorate --all` displays a compact view of branch relationships. `git branch --merged main` can help identify branches already merged into main, while `git merge-base --is-ancestor A B` can test whether one commit is an ancestor of another.\n\nThese commands are useful when a merge appears unexpected, when a branch has diverged, or when a developer needs to determine whether a commit is already included in a target branch. Inspect the graph before performing a history rewrite or deleting a branch. A clear understanding of ancestry prevents accidental duplicate integration and reduces the risk of losing track of commits."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-2-topic-8-section-8",
        "title": "Choosing Merge Strategies for Feature, Release, and Hotfix Workflows",
        "content": "Different branch types may require different integration decisions. Short-lived feature branches often work well with squash merging when the team wants one meaningful commit per completed feature. Teams that need to preserve the full feature development history may prefer merge commits. Rebase can be useful for keeping a feature branch current before integration, provided shared history is handled safely.\n\nRelease branches often need traceable integration points because they support testing, stabilization, and production delivery. A merge commit can make the integration of a release fix visible. Hotfix branches require particular care because their changes may need to reach both the production branch and the ongoing development branch. The team should define how the fix is propagated so that it is not accidentally omitted or applied twice.\n\nA branching policy should explain the permitted merge methods for each branch type, the required approvals, and the expected test evidence. Do not let individual contributors choose inconsistent methods for critical branches. Document exceptions for urgent production fixes and verify that the resulting commit history clearly identifies what was delivered and where it was integrated."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-2-topic-8-section-9",
        "title": "Merge Strategy Effects on Rollback, Revert, and Release Traceability",
        "content": "The selected merge method affects how teams investigate and reverse changes. A squash merge creates one consolidated commit for the pull request, which can make reverting the feature relatively straightforward when the commit is isolated. A fast-forward merge advances the branch to existing commits, so reverting may require selecting one or more commits from the integrated sequence.\n\nA merge commit records two parent histories. Reverting a merge commit requires choosing the mainline parent with the `-m` option because Git must know which parent represents the history to retain. For example, `git revert -m 1 <merge-commit>` commonly reverts a merge relative to its first parent, but the correct parent must be verified for the actual repository graph. Reverting a merge does not erase the original commits from history.\n\nRelease traceability also depends on tags, build identifiers, artifact digests, and deployment records. A merge commit alone does not prove which artifact reached production. Preserve the source commit and artifact metadata in the delivery pipeline so that an incident responder can identify the exact code version and determine a safe recovery action."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-2-topic-8-section-10",
        "title": "Merge Queue, Concurrent Pull Requests, and Integration Ordering",
        "content": "When many pull requests target the same protected branch, independently passing checks can become outdated as other changes are merged. A pull request may pass CI against one version of main but fail when combined with a newer version. Merge queues address this integration problem by managing the order in which eligible pull requests are tested and merged, subject to the repository's supported configuration.\n\nA merge queue can create temporary integration candidates that combine queued changes with the latest target branch state. Required checks run against the candidate so that the repository validates the combined result rather than relying only on older individual pull request results. If a candidate fails, the queue can prevent that change from entering the target branch while allowing the queue to continue according to its configuration.\n\nMerge queues are especially useful in repositories with high pull request volume and strict CI requirements. They require reliable workflows, correct required-check configuration, and an understanding of how queue events trigger automation. Test workflows against the relevant merge-group event where required by the platform. Monitor queue failures and avoid weakening branch protections simply to reduce waiting time."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-2-topic-8-section-11",
        "title": "Command-Line Merge Operations, Inspection, and Safe Abort",
        "content": "Git provides commands to inspect and control merge operations. Start with `git status` to understand the working tree and `git branch --show-current` to confirm the current branch. Use `git fetch origin` to update remote-tracking references before comparing branches. Inspect differences with `git log --oneline --graph --decorate --all` and `git diff main...feature/search` where the comparison is appropriate.\n\nTo merge a branch, check out the intended target branch and run `git merge feature/search`. Use `git merge --ff-only feature/search` when only fast-forward integration is permitted. Use `git merge --no-ff feature/search` when a merge commit is required. If a merge is in progress and should be abandoned before completion, `git merge --abort` attempts to restore the pre-merge state.\n\nDo not run merge commands without confirming the target branch and working tree state. Commit or safely preserve unrelated local changes first. After a successful merge, inspect the resulting graph, run relevant tests, and push only when the repository policy allows it. If a command fails, read the exact error and inspect repository status before attempting another operation."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-2-topic-8-section-12",
        "title": "Production Merge Policy, Validation, and Practical Integration Exercise",
        "content": "A production merge policy defines which merge methods are permitted, when each method should be used, and what evidence is required before integration. It should align with branch protection, pull request approvals, required CI checks, release traceability, and rollback procedures. The policy should also explain how teams handle shared feature branches, urgent fixes, and merge queue failures.\n\nPractical exercise: create a test repository with main and a feature branch. Add commits to the feature branch and compare a fast-forward merge with a merge commit using `git log --graph --oneline --all`. Next, create divergent commits on both branches and observe why a fast-forward is no longer possible. Try `git merge --ff-only` and verify that Git refuses the operation rather than silently creating another merge type.\n\nCreate another feature branch with multiple commits and compare squash integration with a normal merge. Inspect the resulting commit graph and practice reverting the integrated change in a disposable test branch. Finally, configure the repository's permitted pull request merge methods, verify required checks, and document the chosen strategy for feature, release, and hotfix branches. Never conduct destructive history experiments on a production branch."
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "topicTitle": "Merge Conflict Resolution",
    "sections": [
      {
        "id": "stage-2-topic-9-section-1",
        "title": "Git Merge Conflicts and Why They Occur",
        "content": "A Git merge conflict occurs when Git cannot automatically combine changes from different branches. Git can merge many independent edits without human involvement, but it cannot safely decide between certain competing changes. A conflict is not necessarily a sign that code is incorrect. It indicates that the intended final result requires a developer to make an explicit decision.\n\nA common example occurs when two branches modify the same lines of a file differently. One developer changes a database connection method while another modifies the same method to support a new configuration format. Git may be unable to determine which implementation should remain. Conflicts can also occur when one branch deletes a file while another modifies it, when files are renamed differently, or when changes overlap in complex ways.\n\nConflict resolution is a correctness responsibility, not just a command-line operation. Developers must understand the purpose of both changes, preserve required behavior, and run relevant tests after resolving the conflict. Blindly selecting one side can remove important functionality or introduce production defects. Always inspect the conflict context before marking it resolved."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-2-topic-9-section-2",
        "title": "Conflict Types, File-Level Changes, and Git Conflict Stages",
        "content": "Git conflicts can involve different types of changes. Content conflicts happen when competing edits overlap in the same file. Modify/delete conflicts occur when one branch changes a file while another deletes it. Rename conflicts can occur when branches rename the same file differently or when a rename overlaps with another modification. Binary files may also require manual selection because Git cannot combine their contents like ordinary text.\n\nDuring an unresolved merge, Git records conflict information in the index. For a typical unmerged path, the index can contain stage 1 for the common ancestor, stage 2 for the current branch side, and stage 3 for the incoming branch side. These entries represent the versions Git needs to help the developer resolve the conflict.\n\nUse `git status` to identify unmerged paths. Use `git ls-files -u` to inspect unmerged index entries. For a particular file, commands such as `git show :1:path/to/file`, `git show :2:path/to/file`, and `git show :3:path/to/file` can display the base, current-side, and incoming-side versions when those stages exist. Not every conflict has all three stages. Understand the conflict type before choosing a resolution."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-2-topic-9-section-3",
        "title": "Reading Conflict Markers and Understanding Both Changes",
        "content": "When Git reports a text conflict, it may insert conflict markers into the affected file. A typical conflict block begins with `<<<<<<< HEAD`, contains the current branch version, separates the alternatives with `=======`, and ends with `>>>>>>> feature-branch`. The exact labels depend on the operation and configuration.\n\nThe content between the opening marker and separator represents one side of the conflict, while the content between the separator and closing marker represents the other side. These markers are temporary conflict annotations, not valid application logic. The developer must edit the file into the intended final state and remove all conflict markers before completing the merge.\n\nDo not assume that the current side is always correct or that the incoming side should always win. Compare both versions with the common ancestor and inspect the surrounding code. Determine whether both changes are required and whether their combined behavior is compatible. A correct resolution may retain one version, combine both implementations, or redesign the affected code to preserve the original intent of both changes."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-2-topic-9-section-4",
        "title": "Conflict Diagnosis Using Git Status, Diff, and Log Commands",
        "content": "Effective conflict resolution starts with diagnosis. Run `git status` to understand whether the repository is in a merge, rebase, or cherry-pick operation and to list unresolved files. Do not begin editing until you know which operation is active. The correct continuation or abort command depends on that operation.\n\nUse `git diff --name-only --diff-filter=U` to list paths that remain unmerged. Use `git diff` to inspect unresolved changes and `git log --oneline --graph --decorate --all` to understand branch history. For a particular path, inspect the base and both sides when available. Compare related files and configuration because a conflict in one file may depend on changes made elsewhere.\n\nRecord which files are affected and identify the behavior each branch was intended to introduce. If the conflict involves application code, check callers, interfaces, tests, and dependencies. If it involves infrastructure or deployment configuration, inspect the affected environment and resource relationships. Diagnosis should produce a clear resolution plan before changes are staged."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-2-topic-9-section-5",
        "title": "Manual Conflict Resolution and Safe File Editing",
        "content": "Manual conflict resolution means editing an affected file to create the correct combined result. Open each conflicted file in an editor and inspect the complete conflict block together with surrounding code. Decide whether to retain the current branch implementation, the incoming implementation, or a compatible combination of both. Preserve required imports, function signatures, validation rules, error handling, and configuration behavior.\n\nRemove every conflict marker from the final file. Do not simply delete one side without understanding its purpose. For example, if one branch adds input validation and another adds structured logging, the correct result may need both changes. Check formatting, syntax, and consistency with neighboring code after editing.\n\nAfter resolving a file, inspect it using `git diff -- path/to/file`. Confirm that the final result contains no accidental deletion or duplicated logic. Only then mark it resolved with `git add path/to/file`. Staging tells Git that the file has been resolved; it does not prove that the implementation is correct. Run the relevant tests before completing the merge or rebase."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-2-topic-9-section-6",
        "title": "Choosing Current, Incoming, or Combined Changes Safely",
        "content": "Git provides ways to select one side of a conflicted file, but the correct choice depends on the operation and the intended behavior. During a normal merge, `git checkout --ours path/to/file` selects the version from the current branch, while `git checkout --theirs path/to/file` selects the incoming branch version. Modern Git also provides equivalent restore workflows for supported operations.\n\nThese options replace the conflicted file content with one side's version. They should not be used as a shortcut when both branches contain required changes. For example, choosing the incoming version may remove a security validation introduced on the current branch. Choosing the current version may discard an essential database migration from the incoming branch.\n\nFor complex conflicts, inspect both versions first and construct a combined result manually. The meaning of ours and theirs can be especially confusing during a rebase because the operation replays commits onto a new base. Always confirm the active operation and inspect the actual file content before using side-selection commands. After selection, review the diff and run appropriate tests."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-2-topic-9-section-7",
        "title": "Resolving Conflicts During Rebase and Cherry-Pick Operations",
        "content": "Conflicts can occur during rebase and cherry-pick operations as well as normal merges. During a rebase, Git replays commits onto a new base. If a replayed commit cannot be applied cleanly, Git pauses and asks the developer to resolve the conflict. After editing the affected files, stage the resolved paths and run `git rebase --continue` to proceed.\n\nIf the rebase should be abandoned, `git rebase --abort` attempts to return the branch to its original pre-rebase state. Use `git rebase --skip` only when the current commit is intentionally unnecessary and its changes are safely accounted for elsewhere. Skipping a commit without understanding its purpose can silently omit required functionality.\n\nDuring a cherry-pick conflict, inspect the operation and affected commit, resolve the files, stage them, and run `git cherry-pick --continue`. To abandon the operation, use `git cherry-pick --abort` when appropriate. Do not use `git merge --continue` for every conflict situation. Match the continuation or abort command to the operation reported by Git."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-2-topic-9-section-8",
        "title": "Rename, Delete-Modify, Binary, and Configuration Conflicts",
        "content": "Not all merge conflicts are simple overlapping text edits. A modify/delete conflict requires deciding whether the file should remain with the modifications or be removed. A rename conflict may require choosing the correct destination path and preserving changes made before or after the rename. Git may report both paths involved, so inspect repository status carefully.\n\nBinary files such as images, compiled assets, and certain document formats usually cannot be combined line by line. The team may need to choose one version, regenerate the asset from its source, or use a specialized tool. Avoid manually editing binary data with a text editor.\n\nConfiguration conflicts deserve additional attention because the same setting may have different values for development, staging, and production. Examples include Kubernetes manifests, Docker Compose files, CI workflow YAML, Terraform variables, and application configuration. Preserve environment-specific intent, validate syntax, and check whether both branches introduced required fields. Use the relevant validator or deployment plan before accepting the result."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-2-topic-9-section-9",
        "title": "Conflict Resolution in IDEs and Visual Merge Tools",
        "content": "Visual merge tools provide side-by-side views of the common ancestor, current branch, incoming branch, and final result. They help developers understand how individual lines changed and can make complicated conflicts easier to resolve. Editors and IDEs such as Visual Studio Code can display conflict blocks and provide actions to accept the current change, accept the incoming change, accept both changes, or compare versions.\n\nThese actions are editing conveniences, not correctness guarantees. Accepting both changes may create duplicate imports, repeated configuration entries, conflicting function definitions, or invalid syntax. Accepting one side may remove a required fix. Review the complete final file rather than relying on the editor's conflict indicators alone.\n\nFor large or high-risk conflicts, use a dedicated merge tool configured through Git's merge-tool settings. A team can standardize tools and document the expected workflow. After using a visual tool, inspect the result with `git diff`, verify that no conflict markers remain, stage the file, and run the relevant automated checks."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-2-topic-9-section-10",
        "title": "Testing Resolved Conflicts and Preventing Regression Defects",
        "content": "A resolved conflict is only a syntactically integrated file; it is not automatically a correct implementation. The developer must verify that the combined result preserves the required behavior from both branches. Start with formatting and syntax validation, then run focused tests for the changed functionality. Follow with unit tests, integration tests, and broader CI checks according to the risk of the change.\n\nFor application code, verify interfaces, error handling, security checks, and edge cases. For database changes, confirm migration ordering and compatibility with existing data. For infrastructure configuration, run the appropriate validation and plan commands before applying changes. For Kubernetes manifests, use schema and policy validation before deploying to a shared environment.\n\nReview the final diff and compare it with the original branch intentions. Ask the relevant code owners to inspect high-risk resolutions. If tests reveal a regression, correct the implementation before completing the operation. Never treat the disappearance of conflict markers or a successful `git add` as sufficient evidence that the conflict was correctly resolved."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-2-topic-9-section-11",
        "title": "Aborting, Recovering, and Safely Restarting Failed Operations",
        "content": "Sometimes the safest response to a complicated conflict is to stop and restart the integration with a clearer plan. Before aborting, inspect `git status` and preserve any valuable uncommitted work. If the active operation is a merge, `git merge --abort` attempts to restore the state from before the merge began. If it is a rebase, use `git rebase --abort`. For a cherry-pick, use `git cherry-pick --abort`.\n\nDo not assume that every abort operation can recover unrelated local changes that were present before the operation. Git may not be able to reconstruct changes that were overwritten or not safely preserved. Before risky work, commit or stash unrelated changes and consider creating a temporary backup branch or patch. Avoid using `git reset --hard` as a generic conflict fix because it can discard local work.\n\nIf the branch becomes difficult to recover, inspect `git reflog` to find previous branch positions and commit references. Recovery should be performed carefully, preferably on a new branch or in a disposable worktree. Confirm the expected commit graph before force-pushing or changing a shared remote branch."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-2-topic-9-section-12",
        "title": "Production Conflict Scenarios and Practical Resolution Lab",
        "content": "Production conflicts often involve more than two developers editing the same source file. A feature branch may change an API contract while another branch updates its client. A database migration may conflict with a release fix. A Kubernetes deployment manifest may contain different replica counts or resource limits introduced by separate environment changes. Resolving such conflicts requires understanding dependencies and deployment impact, not merely selecting lines.\n\nPractical exercise: create a test repository with main and two feature branches. Modify the same function differently on each branch and attempt to merge them. Inspect the conflict markers, compare both implementations, create a combined solution, stage the file, and complete the merge. Run tests that verify both behaviors remain available.\n\nRepeat the exercise with a modify/delete conflict and a rebase conflict. Practice aborting each operation using its correct command. Finally, resolve a YAML configuration conflict and validate the resulting file. Record the original cause, affected paths, resolution decision, tests executed, and reviewer approval. Never experiment with destructive conflict recovery on a production branch."
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "topicTitle": "GitHub Webhooks",
    "sections": [
      {
        "id": "stage-2-topic-10-section-1",
        "title": "Webhook Fundamentals and Event-Driven Integration",
        "content": "A webhook is an HTTP callback mechanism that allows one application to notify another application when a specific event occurs. Instead of repeatedly polling GitHub to check whether something changed, an external system can receive an HTTP request when GitHub generates a configured event. This supports event-driven automation and reduces unnecessary API polling.\n\nFor example, when a developer opens a pull request, GitHub can send a webhook notification to an internal integration service. That service can validate the request, inspect the event type, and trigger an approved workflow such as updating a dashboard, notifying a team, or creating an external tracking record.\n\nWebhooks are useful for connecting GitHub with CI/CD platforms, incident management tools, chat systems, security platforms, and custom applications. A webhook notification should be treated as an event signal, not as proof that a deployment or business operation completed successfully. The receiving application must validate the request, process the event safely, and report its own execution result."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-2-topic-10-section-2",
        "title": "Webhook Architecture, Registration, and Delivery Flow",
        "content": "A GitHub webhook integration has three main parts: an event producer, an HTTP delivery endpoint, and a receiving application. GitHub acts as the event producer. A repository or organization administrator configures the webhook URL, selected event subscriptions, content type, and optional secret. When a subscribed event occurs, GitHub prepares a request containing event metadata and a payload.\n\nGitHub sends an HTTP POST request to the configured endpoint. The receiving application accepts the request, validates its authenticity, identifies the event type, and processes the payload. The application should return an appropriate HTTP response promptly. If processing is expensive, it can place the event into a durable queue and perform the work asynchronously.\n\nA typical delivery flow is: developer opens a pull request, GitHub generates the pull-request event, the configured webhook receives the notification, an integration service validates the signature, and a worker updates an external system. Keep the receiver reachable through an approved network path and ensure that the endpoint can handle retries, temporary outages, and concurrent deliveries."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-2-topic-10-section-3",
        "title": "Webhook Configuration, Content Types, and Event Subscriptions",
        "content": "GitHub repository webhooks are configured in repository settings under the webhook management area, subject to the user's permissions. Administrators specify the payload URL, content type, optional shared secret, and which events should trigger delivery. Common content types include JSON and form-encoded payload formats, depending on the integration requirements.\n\nEvent subscriptions should follow the principle of least privilege. Select only the events required by the receiver. Examples include push, pull request, issue, release, deployment, workflow-related, and repository events. Exact event availability and payload details depend on GitHub's webhook event model and the webhook scope.\n\nFor a CI integration, a push event may be sufficient to start a build when code reaches a branch. A pull request event can support validation before merge. A release event can notify an artifact or deployment system that a release has been published. Avoid subscribing to every event without a clear requirement because unnecessary deliveries increase processing load and make monitoring harder. Document each webhook's owner, purpose, target endpoint, event list, and secret rotation procedure."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-2-topic-10-section-4",
        "title": "Webhook Payload Structure, Headers, and Event Identification",
        "content": "A webhook payload contains structured data describing the event that occurred. The payload may include repository information, sender details, branch references, commit identifiers, pull request metadata, issue information, or release details depending on the event. Receivers should parse only the fields required for their business logic and should tolerate optional or newly introduced fields.\n\nGitHub includes headers that help the receiver identify and correlate a delivery. Important examples include `X-GitHub-Event`, which identifies the event type, and `X-GitHub-Delivery`, which provides a delivery identifier. Signature-related headers such as `X-Hub-Signature-256` are used when a webhook secret is configured. The exact header set depends on the delivery and platform behavior.\n\nThe receiver should use the event header to select the appropriate handler rather than guessing the event type from arbitrary payload fields. Store the delivery identifier for diagnostics and duplicate detection. Avoid logging the full payload indiscriminately because it may contain personal information or sensitive repository metadata. Define a version-tolerant payload parser and test it against representative event examples."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-2-topic-10-section-5",
        "title": "Webhook Signature Validation and HMAC-SHA256 Security",
        "content": "Webhook receivers must verify that incoming requests genuinely originate from the expected sender. GitHub supports a shared webhook secret and an HMAC-SHA256 signature delivered through the `X-Hub-Signature-256` header. The receiver calculates the expected signature using the exact raw request body and configured secret, then compares it with the supplied signature using a constant-time comparison.\n\nSignature verification must happen before the application trusts or processes the payload. Do not parse and reserialize JSON before calculating the signature because whitespace or property formatting changes can produce a different byte sequence. Read the raw request body, calculate the HMAC digest, format it as expected, and compare it safely.\n\nKeep webhook secrets in an approved secret manager or protected environment configuration. Never hardcode the secret in source code, commit it to Git, or expose it in application logs. Rotate the secret through a controlled process that updates GitHub and the receiver consistently. Reject requests with missing, malformed, or invalid signatures. Also protect the endpoint against replay and duplicate processing through delivery tracking and idempotent handlers."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-2-topic-10-section-6",
        "title": "Webhook Receiver Implementation and HTTP Endpoint Design",
        "content": "A webhook receiver is an HTTP endpoint that accepts incoming POST requests and performs validation and event handling. It should enforce a reasonable request-size limit, accept only expected HTTP methods, validate the signature, identify the event type, and reject malformed or unsupported requests safely. The endpoint should return a response within the sender's delivery timeout expectations.\n\nA simple implementation can use an API route in a Node.js or Next.js service, an Express application, a Java Spring Boot controller, or another HTTP framework. The application must preserve access to the raw request body for signature verification. Framework middleware that automatically consumes or transforms the body must be configured carefully.\n\nSeparate request validation from business processing. The HTTP layer should authenticate and validate the event, record its delivery ID, and enqueue work. A background worker can then call external APIs, trigger approved jobs, or update systems. This design reduces request latency and avoids losing work when a downstream service is temporarily unavailable. Add health checks, structured logs, metrics, and clear error handling to support production operations."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-2-topic-10-section-7",
        "title": "Reliable Event Processing, Retries, and Duplicate Delivery Handling",
        "content": "Webhook delivery is a distributed integration and must tolerate network failures, timeouts, temporary receiver outages, and duplicate notifications. A receiver should not assume that every event arrives exactly once or in the same order in which related changes occurred. Design handlers to be idempotent so that processing the same logical event more than once does not create duplicate deployments, tickets, or records.\n\nUse the delivery identifier as one input to duplicate detection. Store processed identifiers in a durable database or queue-backed state store with an appropriate retention policy. For business operations, also use a stable domain identifier such as a pull request number, release identifier, or deployment key. A repeated event should return a safe result without repeating irreversible side effects.\n\nGitHub may retry failed deliveries under supported delivery behavior, and administrators can inspect and redeliver webhook events through available tooling. Do not rely exclusively on sender retries for guaranteed processing. Implement internal retry policies for transient downstream failures, bounded exponential backoff, dead-letter handling, and operational alerts. Distinguish permanent validation errors from temporary service failures."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-2-topic-10-section-8",
        "title": "Webhook Event Ordering, Queues, and Asynchronous Workers",
        "content": "Events can arrive close together, be processed concurrently, or reach the receiver in an order that does not match the order of related repository changes. A receiver should not assume that receiving a later notification means all earlier events have already been processed. This is particularly important when an integration updates deployment state, release records, or external workflow systems.\n\nA durable queue separates webhook acceptance from longer-running business operations. The HTTP endpoint validates the request, stores the event or a safe reference to it, and acknowledges acceptance. Workers then process queued items with controlled concurrency and retry behavior. Queue records should include delivery IDs, event types, timestamps, and processing state.\n\nWhere ordering matters, partition work using a stable key such as repository and branch, or verify the current source state before performing an action. For example, before deploying a push event, compare the event's commit SHA with the current intended deployment reference. This prevents an older queued event from accidentally deploying code after a newer release has already been selected. Monitor queue depth, processing latency, retry count, and dead-letter volume."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-2-topic-10-section-9",
        "title": "Connecting Webhooks to CI/CD, ChatOps, and External Platforms",
        "content": "Webhooks connect GitHub repository activity to systems that need timely notifications. A push event can notify a build orchestrator that source code changed. A pull request event can update a review dashboard or start a validation pipeline. A release event can notify an artifact repository or deployment coordination service. Issue and discussion events can synchronize project management or support systems.\n\nFor a CI/CD integration, the receiver should validate the event and determine whether the target branch, repository, and commit are eligible for the requested operation. It should not automatically deploy every push to every environment. Production deployment must continue to follow approval, artifact validation, and environment access policies.\n\nChatOps integrations can post pull request updates, deployment notifications, and incident information to team communication platforms. External system calls should use authenticated credentials stored in a secret manager. Apply rate limits, bounded retries, and clear failure handling. Record the source delivery ID and resulting external operation ID so that operators can trace a webhook event through the complete automation chain."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-2-topic-10-section-10",
        "title": "Webhook Monitoring, Delivery Logs, and Operational Observability",
        "content": "Production webhook integrations require observability across both delivery and processing. GitHub's webhook configuration and delivery interface can help administrators inspect recent deliveries, request details, response status, and redelivery options where available. These records are useful for identifying whether GitHub reached the endpoint and what response it received.\n\nThe receiving service should emit structured logs containing the delivery ID, event type, repository identifier, processing outcome, duration, and correlation ID. Avoid logging secrets, authorization headers, or unnecessary payload content. Metrics should include accepted requests, signature failures, HTTP response codes, queue depth, processing latency, retry count, and dead-letter events.\n\nDefine alerts for sustained delivery failures, a growing queue, repeated signature validation errors, and processing delays that affect release workflows. A successful HTTP acknowledgement does not necessarily mean the downstream operation succeeded. Track the event from receipt through queueing, worker execution, and final business outcome. This gives operators a complete view when an event was accepted but an integration action did not finish."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-2-topic-10-section-11",
        "title": "Webhook Troubleshooting, Testing, and Security Hardening",
        "content": "Webhook failures can originate from DNS problems, TLS certificate errors, firewall restrictions, endpoint routing, invalid secrets, request parsing, application exceptions, or downstream service outages. Start by inspecting the delivery attempt and its HTTP response. Confirm that the endpoint is reachable over HTTPS, accepts POST requests, and returns the expected response within the delivery timeout.\n\nA signature mismatch may occur when the wrong secret is configured, the raw request body was modified, or the signature comparison uses the wrong encoding. A successful delivery followed by missing business activity may indicate that the receiver acknowledged the request but failed to enqueue or process the event. Inspect correlated application logs and queue records rather than repeatedly redelivering without diagnosis.\n\nTest using representative event payloads and GitHub's supported webhook testing or redelivery capabilities. Validate invalid signatures, malformed payloads, oversized requests, duplicate delivery IDs, unavailable dependencies, and worker crashes. Harden the endpoint with HTTPS, signature verification, least-privilege credentials, request limits, rate controls, dependency isolation, and secret rotation. Treat every incoming payload as untrusted input."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-2-topic-10-section-12",
        "title": "Production Webhook Architecture and Practical Integration Lab",
        "content": "A production webhook architecture should include a secure HTTPS endpoint, signature verification, event filtering, durable event storage or queuing, idempotent processing, observability, and controlled downstream credentials. The design should document event ownership, delivery expectations, duplicate handling, retry policy, failure recovery, and the process for rotating webhook secrets.\n\nPractical exercise: configure a test GitHub repository webhook pointing to a controlled development endpoint. Subscribe only to push and pull request events and configure a strong shared secret. Generate a test event and inspect the request headers and payload. Implement raw-body HMAC-SHA256 validation and reject a request with an invalid signature.\n\nNext, persist the delivery ID and event metadata before acknowledging the request. Simulate a duplicate delivery and verify that the business action is not repeated. Place the event into a queue and process it with a worker. Simulate a temporary downstream outage, confirm retry behavior, and inspect the final processing status. Finally, document logs, alerts, secret rotation, endpoint ownership, and the recovery procedure. Never expose a production webhook endpoint or secret during testing."
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "topicTitle": "GitHub Actions Integration",
    "sections": [
      {
        "title": "GitHub Actions Architecture and Execution Model",
        "content": "GitHub Actions is GitHub's automation platform for running software workflows directly from repository events. It allows teams to automate continuous integration, testing, packaging, security checks, and deployment without maintaining a separate automation server for every project. A workflow is defined in a YAML file stored under the repository's .github/workflows directory. GitHub detects these files and evaluates their event triggers whenever relevant repository activity occurs. A workflow contains one or more jobs, and each job contains steps that execute commands or reusable actions. Jobs normally run on separate runners, which means files and environment changes are not automatically shared between jobs. The workflow engine evaluates dependencies, permissions, conditions, and concurrency before scheduling work. Understanding this execution model helps engineers design predictable pipelines instead of treating a workflow as one long shell script. In production, teams should separate validation, build, security scanning, and deployment into clear jobs with explicit dependencies. This makes failures easier to identify, allows independent work to run in parallel, and reduces unnecessary deployment risk.",
        "id": "stage-2-topic-11-section-1"
      },
      {
        "title": "Workflow YAML Structure, Syntax, and Repository Organization",
        "content": "GitHub Actions workflows are YAML files placed inside .github/workflows, and each file defines an automation process. A workflow commonly includes a name, an on section for event triggers, permissions, environment variables, concurrency settings, and a jobs section. Each job specifies a runner through runs-on and contains steps that use an action or execute a shell command. The name field provides a readable label in the Actions interface, while the YAML filename identifies the workflow file in the repository. Teams should use descriptive filenames such as ci.yml, security-scan.yml, and deploy-production.yml rather than placing every activity into one enormous workflow. YAML indentation is significant: incorrect nesting can change the meaning of a workflow or make GitHub reject it. The uses property references an action, while run executes commands in the runner shell. The with property passes inputs to an action, and env defines environment variables. Workflow files should be reviewed like application code, validated before merging, and protected through repository rules. A clean organization makes automation easier to understand, maintain, troubleshoot, and reuse across multiple services.",
        "id": "stage-2-topic-11-section-2"
      },
      {
        "title": "Workflow Events, Trigger Filters, and Execution Conditions",
        "content": "The on section determines which GitHub events can start a workflow. Common triggers include push, pull_request, workflow_dispatch, schedule, release, and workflow_call. A push trigger can be restricted to selected branches or file paths, while pull_request can be configured for specific target branches and activity types. Branch filters help prevent unrelated changes from starting expensive pipelines, and path filters can limit execution to services affected by a change. Manual execution through workflow_dispatch is useful for controlled operational tasks, while schedule uses cron expressions for periodic automation. The if expression can conditionally execute jobs or steps based on contexts such as github.ref, github.event_name, needs, and success or failure status. Trigger filters and execution conditions solve different problems: filters determine whether an event starts a workflow, whereas conditions decide whether a particular job or step runs after the workflow has started. Engineers must consider fork pull requests and untrusted contributions when designing triggers because these events can have different token and secret access restrictions. Correct trigger design reduces unnecessary builds while ensuring important changes still receive required validation.",
        "id": "stage-2-topic-11-section-3"
      },
      {
        "title": "Jobs, Steps, Dependencies, and Parallel Pipeline Execution",
        "content": "A GitHub Actions workflow is composed of jobs, and each job contains an ordered sequence of steps. A job runs on a specified GitHub-hosted or self-hosted runner and normally receives a clean execution environment. Steps within the same job share the runner workspace, so a repository checkout, generated build output, and temporary files can be used by later steps in that job. Different jobs are isolated and should exchange required files through artifacts or other explicitly configured storage. The needs property defines job dependencies. For example, a test job can depend on a build job, and a deployment job can depend on both build and security validation. Jobs without dependencies can run in parallel, reducing overall pipeline duration. The if property supports conditional execution, and continue-on-error can be used carefully for non-blocking checks. A production pipeline should avoid making deployment depend on optional informational jobs unless that dependency is intentional. Use meaningful job identifiers and names so engineers can quickly understand which stage failed. Explicit dependencies, clear boundaries, and controlled parallelism improve reliability and make pipeline behavior easier to reason about during incidents.",
        "id": "stage-2-topic-11-section-4"
      },
      {
        "title": "GitHub-Hosted and Self-Hosted Runners",
        "content": "A runner is the machine or execution environment that processes a GitHub Actions job. GitHub-hosted runners are maintained by GitHub and provide commonly used operating-system images, while self-hosted runners are infrastructure managed by the organization. The runs-on property selects the runner environment, such as ubuntu-latest, windows-latest, macos-latest, or a custom self-hosted label. GitHub-hosted runners are convenient for standard build and test workloads because teams do not need to manage the underlying machine. Self-hosted runners are useful when workflows require private network access, specialized hardware, custom software, or controlled infrastructure. They also introduce operational responsibilities: patching, isolation, capacity, monitoring, credential protection, and cleanup. A self-hosted runner should not execute untrusted pull-request code with access to sensitive internal systems. Ephemeral runners are preferable for many production workloads because a fresh machine is provisioned for a job and discarded afterward. Teams should choose runner types according to workload requirements, security boundaries, cost, and execution time. Runner labels, access groups, and network restrictions should be managed deliberately rather than granting every repository unrestricted access.",
        "id": "stage-2-topic-11-section-5"
      },
      {
        "title": "Actions Marketplace, Version Pinning, and Reusable Workflows",
        "content": "GitHub Actions can use prebuilt actions to perform tasks such as checking out source code, configuring a language runtime, caching dependencies, uploading artifacts, and deploying applications. An action is referenced through the uses property and may come from a repository, a published release, or a supported container action. Teams should inspect an action's source, maintainer activity, permissions, dependencies, and security history before adopting it. Pinning third-party actions to a full commit SHA provides stronger supply-chain integrity than relying only on a movable version tag. Reusable workflows allow organizations to define common CI or deployment logic once and call it from other workflow files using workflow_call. This helps standardize build conventions across repositories while reducing duplicated YAML. Composite actions package multiple steps into a reusable unit, whereas reusable workflows share an entire job or workflow structure. These mechanisms serve different levels of reuse and should not be confused. Central platform teams can maintain approved workflow templates, define required inputs, and document supported versions. Reuse improves consistency, but shared workflows must be versioned, tested, and updated carefully because a change can affect many dependent repositories.",
        "id": "stage-2-topic-11-section-6"
      },
      {
        "title": "Build Artifacts, Dependency Caching, and Cross-Job Data",
        "content": "Build artifacts and dependency caches solve different pipeline problems. An artifact is an output that a workflow intentionally preserves, such as a compiled application package, test report, coverage file, or deployment bundle. Artifacts can be uploaded by one job and downloaded by another job or retained for later inspection, subject to configured retention policies. A cache stores reusable dependencies or intermediate data to reduce repeated download and build work. For example, a Java project can cache Maven dependencies, while a Node.js project can cache package-manager data. Cache keys should include relevant lockfile hashes, operating-system information, and runtime versions so incompatible dependencies are not restored accidentally. A cache is an optimization and must never be the only source of a required production artifact. A reliable pipeline should build a versioned artifact once, validate it, and promote that same artifact between environments rather than rebuilding different binaries for each environment. This improves traceability and reduces differences between tested and deployed software. Teams should also consider artifact size, retention, access controls, integrity, and cleanup when designing storage policies.",
        "id": "stage-2-topic-11-section-7"
      },
      {
        "title": "Workflow Permissions, GITHUB_TOKEN, and Least-Privilege Security",
        "content": "GitHub Actions workflows receive a GITHUB_TOKEN that can authenticate supported operations against the repository and GitHub APIs. Its permissions should be limited to the minimum required by each workflow. The permissions key can be declared at workflow level and narrowed further at job level where appropriate. Examples include contents: read for checking out source code and contents: write when a trusted release job must publish a release or update repository content. Pull-request workflows should not receive write permissions unless there is a carefully justified requirement. Teams should review the permissions of every third-party action because an action executes within the job's security context and may access available credentials or files. Forked pull requests have additional restrictions intended to protect repository secrets and write access. Separate trusted deployment workflows from untrusted validation workflows, and avoid executing unreviewed code in privileged contexts. Use environment protection rules and short-lived identity federation where possible instead of distributing long-lived cloud credentials. Security reviews should examine token scope, action pinning, runner isolation, secret exposure, and the exact events that can start privileged jobs. Least privilege limits the damage that can occur if a workflow or dependency is compromised.",
        "id": "stage-2-topic-11-section-8"
      },
      {
        "title": "Environment Deployments, Approvals, and Deployment Protection",
        "content": "GitHub Actions environments represent deployment targets such as development, staging, and production. A deployment job can declare an environment, allowing repository administrators to configure environment-specific protection rules and secrets. Protection features can include required reviewers, deployment branch restrictions, wait timers, and other supported controls depending on the repository plan and configuration. A production deployment should be separated from ordinary pull-request validation so that untrusted changes cannot automatically access production credentials. Required reviewers provide a human approval point before a protected deployment proceeds. Branch restrictions help ensure only approved branches or tags can deploy to a particular environment. Environment secrets are made available to the job only after the applicable protection requirements have been satisfied. Teams should use clear environment names, document who can approve deployments, and record the artifact version being promoted. Approval is not a replacement for automated testing: build validation, security scanning, and deployment checks should complete before a production approval is requested. This approach creates a controlled release path with traceable authorization and fewer accidental production changes.",
        "id": "stage-2-topic-11-section-9"
      },
      {
        "title": "Cloud Deployment with OIDC and Short-Lived Credentials",
        "content": "OpenID Connect, commonly called OIDC, allows a GitHub Actions workflow to obtain a short-lived identity token and exchange it with a cloud provider for temporary credentials. This can remove the need to store long-lived cloud access keys as repository secrets. A workflow that needs OIDC must request the appropriate id-token permission, commonly id-token: write, and the cloud identity provider must be configured to trust the expected GitHub token claims. Trust policies should restrict the repository, branch, tag, environment, or other relevant identity attributes rather than accepting tokens from every workflow. The workflow then uses a supported cloud authentication action or provider-specific exchange process to obtain temporary access. Those temporary credentials should have narrowly scoped permissions for the deployment task. OIDC does not automatically make a deployment secure: an overly broad trust policy or an overprivileged cloud role can still create serious exposure. Teams should validate the issuer, audience, subject conditions, role permissions, and environment restrictions. This pattern is useful for deployments to AWS, Azure, and Google Cloud because it reduces credential rotation work and limits the lifetime of exposed credentials.",
        "id": "stage-2-topic-11-section-10"
      },
      {
        "title": "Workflow Concurrency, Matrix Builds, and Pipeline Optimization",
        "content": "GitHub Actions provides execution controls that help teams balance speed, resource usage, and deployment safety. Matrix strategies run a job across multiple combinations, such as supported language versions, operating systems, or database versions. This is useful for compatibility testing, but a large matrix can create many jobs and increase execution cost. The fail-fast option controls whether remaining matrix jobs are cancelled after a failure, while max-parallel limits simultaneous executions. Concurrency groups can prevent multiple runs from performing conflicting work at the same time. For example, a deployment workflow can use a concurrency group based on the target environment so that two releases do not update production simultaneously. The cancel-in-progress setting determines whether an older run should be cancelled when a newer run enters the same group; this must be chosen carefully for deployment operations. Other optimization techniques include dependency caching, parallel independent jobs, selective path filters, smaller runner images, and avoiding repeated setup steps. Measure workflow duration and queue time before optimizing. The goal is not simply to make every job parallel, but to shorten feedback time without introducing race conditions or weakening required checks.",
        "id": "stage-2-topic-11-section-11"
      },
      {
        "title": "Production CI/CD Pipeline, Observability, and Troubleshooting Lab",
        "content": "A production GitHub Actions pipeline should connect source changes to a repeatable and auditable release process. A practical design begins with pull-request validation: checkout the source, install pinned dependencies, run formatting and static analysis, execute unit tests, build the application, and perform required security checks. After a change is merged, a trusted workflow can create a versioned artifact, publish it to an approved registry, and deploy it to a non-production environment. Smoke tests and deployment health checks should verify the result before a protected production environment is made available for approval. The same validated artifact should be promoted rather than rebuilt. When troubleshooting, inspect the workflow run summary, event payload, job dependencies, runner selection, step logs, action versions, token permissions, and environment protection status. Common failures include invalid YAML, missing permissions, incorrect branch filters, unavailable runner labels, cache mismatches, expired credentials, and deployment approval requirements. Add meaningful job names, explicit timeouts, clear failure messages, and notifications for critical pipeline failures. A hands-on lab should create a workflow, trigger it with a pull request, inspect its jobs, upload an artifact, and execute a protected deployment using least-privilege permissions.",
        "id": "stage-2-topic-11-section-12"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "topicTitle": "Repository Secrets",
    "sections": [
      {
        "title": "Repository Secrets Fundamentals and Security Purpose",
        "content": "Repository secrets are encrypted values used by automation workflows when they need credentials or other confidential information. Examples include API tokens, deployment credentials, private package registry passwords, signing keys, and application-specific connection credentials. A secret should not be committed to source code, stored in a plain-text configuration file, or printed in workflow logs. GitHub provides secret storage so authorized workflows can reference sensitive values without placing the original value directly in the workflow YAML. A workflow typically accesses a secret through the secrets context and passes it to an action or process through an environment variable or supported input. Secret storage does not make every workflow automatically safe: a workflow that can access a secret may still expose it through malicious code, unsafe shell commands, compromised dependencies, or excessive logging. Engineers must therefore decide which workflows genuinely need each secret and restrict access accordingly. Production credentials should be separated from development credentials, and secrets should be rotated when exposure is suspected or ownership changes. The objective is to reduce accidental disclosure while maintaining controlled access for legitimate automation.",
        "id": "stage-2-topic-12-section-1"
      },
      {
        "title": "Repository, Organization, and Environment Secret Scopes",
        "content": "GitHub supports secrets at different scopes so organizations can control which repositories and deployment environments can use sensitive values. Repository secrets belong to one repository and are suitable for credentials used only by that project's workflows. Organization secrets can be shared with selected repositories or repository groups according to the organization's access policy. This avoids manually duplicating common credentials across many repositories, but the selected repository access list must be reviewed carefully. Environment secrets are associated with a deployment environment such as staging or production and are made available to jobs that reference that environment after applicable protection rules are satisfied. Scope selection should follow the principle of least privilege: a staging workflow should not receive a production database password simply because both workflows exist in the same repository. Teams should avoid using organization-wide secrets for values needed by only one project. They should also document the purpose, owner, scope, consuming workflows, and rotation expectations for every important secret. Correct scope design limits accidental exposure and makes it easier to review which automation processes can access a credential.",
        "id": "stage-2-topic-12-section-2"
      },
      {
        "title": "Creating, Updating, and Managing Repository Secrets",
        "content": "Repository administrators and users with the appropriate permissions can create or update repository secrets through the repository settings interface or supported GitHub CLI commands. In the web interface, open the repository settings, locate the Secrets and variables section, choose Actions, and create a new repository secret with a clear name and its confidential value. Secret names should communicate purpose without revealing the secret itself. For example, AWS_DEPLOY_ROLE or REGISTRY_PASSWORD is more understandable than an unclear name such as VALUE1. When using GitHub CLI, the gh secret set command can create or update a secret, and options can specify the repository or environment scope. Access to the CLI session and the terminal must also be protected because command history, process output, shell tracing, or temporary files can accidentally expose sensitive data. GitHub does not provide a normal workflow mechanism for retrieving the original stored secret value after creation, so teams should maintain authoritative credentials in an approved password manager or secrets vault. Updating a secret should be coordinated with the consuming application or deployment process to avoid unexpected authentication failures. Administrative procedures should record the change without recording the confidential value.",
        "id": "stage-2-topic-12-section-3"
      },
      {
        "title": "Accessing Secrets in Workflow YAML and Runtime Processes",
        "content": "A GitHub Actions workflow can reference an available secret through the secrets context. For example, a step can assign a secret to an environment variable using an expression such as ${{ secrets.REGISTRY_PASSWORD }}. The receiving command or action can then read the environment variable at runtime. This keeps the actual value out of the workflow file, but the value is still available to the process that receives it. Teams should pass a secret only to the specific step that needs it rather than exposing it as a workflow-wide environment variable. Avoid embedding secrets directly into shell command strings, command-line arguments, generated configuration files, or diagnostic output because these locations can be recorded by process listings, logs, artifacts, or debugging tools. A workflow should never print a secret to verify that it exists. Instead, test whether the variable is present and report a safe success or failure message. Secret availability also depends on the triggering event, repository permissions, environment protection rules, and the workflow's execution context. Engineers should verify these rules before designing a deployment workflow that depends on a particular credential.",
        "id": "stage-2-topic-12-section-4"
      },
      {
        "title": "Secrets Context, Environment Variables, and Configuration Values",
        "content": "GitHub Actions provides separate mechanisms for confidential secrets and ordinary configuration values. The secrets context is intended for sensitive values such as passwords, tokens, and private keys. The vars context is intended for non-confidential configuration such as deployment region names, feature settings, service identifiers, and environment labels. A value should not be stored as a secret merely because it is used by a workflow; unnecessary secret classification makes configuration harder to inspect and maintain. Conversely, a password or access token must never be moved into vars for convenience. Environment variables provide a runtime interface between workflow configuration and the commands or tools that execute on the runner. They do not automatically encrypt a value or prevent the receiving process from exposing it. A secure design keeps confidential values in secret storage, ordinary settings in variables, and passes each value only to the process that needs it. Teams should also avoid placing confidential information in workflow names, job names, step names, artifact filenames, or generated reports. Clear naming and a documented separation between secrets and variables make pipeline configuration easier to review and reduce the chance of accidental disclosure.",
        "id": "stage-2-topic-12-section-5"
      },
      {
        "title": "Secret Masking, Log Redaction, and Exposure Limitations",
        "content": "GitHub Actions attempts to redact registered secret values from workflow logs, but masking must not be treated as a complete security boundary. A secret may be exposed if a workflow transforms it, splits it into smaller pieces, encodes it, prints derived values, or sends it to an external service. Debug logging and shell tracing can also reveal information that normal execution would not display. Teams should never deliberately print credentials, use echo statements to inspect them, or include them in exception messages. The workflow command-line interface provides a masking mechanism for additional sensitive values, but masking only reduces accidental display and does not prevent a process from transmitting data elsewhere. Artifacts, test reports, caches, generated configuration files, and deployment logs must also be checked for credential leakage. If a secret may have appeared in logs or artifacts, revoke or rotate the credential first rather than relying only on deleting the visible output. Review retention and access permissions for workflow logs and artifacts. The safest approach is to minimize the number of processes receiving secrets, avoid unnecessary transformations, and use short-lived credentials wherever possible.",
        "id": "stage-2-topic-12-section-6"
      },
      {
        "title": "Secret Access Controls, Workflow Trust, and Pull Request Security",
        "content": "Secret access must be evaluated together with the workflow event that starts a job and the code that the job executes. Pull requests from forks are subject to restrictions that generally prevent ordinary access to repository secrets and write permissions. These restrictions help protect maintainers from untrusted contributions, but workflow authors must understand the event-specific behavior before depending on a secret. The pull_request_target event runs in the base repository context and can have access to elevated permissions or secrets, so checking out and executing untrusted pull-request code in that context can create a serious security vulnerability. A malicious contributor could modify scripts or build files that a privileged workflow later executes. Separate untrusted validation from trusted deployment, avoid executing fork-controlled code in privileged jobs, and require reviewed changes before production credentials become available. Use narrow GITHUB_TOKEN permissions and environment protection rules in addition to secret scoping. Review who can modify workflow files because a change to a workflow can change how credentials are used. Security depends on the complete execution path, not simply on whether a credential is stored in GitHub's encrypted secret facility.",
        "id": "stage-2-topic-12-section-7"
      },
      {
        "title": "Secret Rotation, Revocation, and Credential Lifecycle",
        "content": "Secret rotation replaces an existing credential with a new value to reduce the impact of accidental exposure, personnel changes, expired credentials, or organizational security requirements. A rotation process should identify the credential owner, the systems that accept it, the workflows that consume it, and the expected impact of replacement. For credentials that support overlapping validity, create the new credential first, update the GitHub secret, verify the consuming workflow, and then revoke the old credential. This sequence reduces downtime because the application can transition without a gap in authentication. If a credential is actively compromised, revoke it immediately and prioritize containment over a zero-downtime transition. After updating the secret, run a controlled workflow to confirm that authentication and deployment still work. Do not put the old or new value into ticket comments, logs, or chat messages. Maintain rotation records containing timestamps, owners, affected systems, and verification results without recording secret values. Automated expiration reminders and centralized credential ownership improve reliability. Rotation should be a repeatable operational process rather than an emergency activity performed only after a security incident.",
        "id": "stage-2-topic-12-section-8"
      },
      {
        "title": "External Secrets Managers and Dynamic Credential Retrieval",
        "content": "GitHub repository secrets are useful for workflow credentials, but large organizations may prefer a centralized secrets manager for dynamic credentials, auditing, policy enforcement, and cross-platform access. External systems such as cloud secret managers or enterprise vault platforms can provide secrets to a workflow at runtime after authenticating the workload. OpenID Connect can establish workload identity without requiring a long-lived cloud access key to be stored in the repository. The workflow requests an identity token with the required permission, and the external identity provider validates its claims against a trust policy. The workflow then obtains temporary credentials or retrieves an authorized secret from the external manager. Trust policies should restrict repository identity, branch or tag, deployment environment, and other relevant claims. Access should be read-only and limited to the specific secret paths required by the job. Dynamic credentials can reduce exposure windows because they expire automatically, but they still require secure token handling and appropriate audit controls. Teams should choose between GitHub-managed secrets and external secret retrieval according to operational complexity, compliance requirements, credential lifetime, and the number of systems sharing the same credentials.",
        "id": "stage-2-topic-12-section-9"
      },
      {
        "title": "Secret Usage in Docker, Kubernetes, and Cloud Deployment Pipelines",
        "content": "CI/CD workflows often need credentials to authenticate with container registries, cloud platforms, package repositories, and Kubernetes environments. A Docker build or image-publishing job may need registry authentication, while a deployment job may need permission to update a Kubernetes cluster or cloud service. Credentials should be supplied only to the relevant job and should not be copied into the container image, Dockerfile, build arguments, source repository, or published build artifact. Docker build secrets provide a safer mechanism for making sensitive build-time data available to supported build steps without embedding it into image layers. For Kubernetes deployments, use the cluster's supported identity and secret-management mechanisms rather than committing Kubernetes Secret manifests containing plain-text credentials. For cloud deployment, prefer OIDC-based temporary credentials and narrowly scoped roles over permanent access keys. Separate development, staging, and production credentials and use environment protection for production access. A successful workflow should not leave credentials in generated files, runner workspaces, debug logs, or reusable artifacts. Validate the final image and deployment configuration to confirm that sensitive values have not been packaged into the application.",
        "id": "stage-2-topic-12-section-10"
      },
      {
        "title": "Secret Auditing, Governance, and Incident Response",
        "content": "Secret governance defines who may create, update, approve, and use credentials across repositories and environments. Organizations should maintain an inventory containing the secret name, purpose, owner, scope, consuming workflows, creation date, and rotation policy. The inventory must never contain the actual secret value. Repository administrators should periodically review repository and organization access, environment protection rules, workflow permissions, and the repositories authorized to consume organization secrets. Audit records can help investigate administrative changes and identify suspicious access patterns, subject to the available GitHub plan and audit capabilities. When a suspected disclosure occurs, identify the affected credential, revoke or rotate it, inspect relevant workflow runs and logs, and determine whether the credential was used by an unauthorized party. Review artifacts, caches, external service logs, and deployment activity as part of the investigation. Preserve incident evidence according to organizational policy while preventing further exposure of the credential. After containment, identify the root cause, remove unsafe workflow behavior, narrow permissions, and add preventive checks. Governance is effective when ownership, access review, rotation, and incident response are defined before an emergency occurs.",
        "id": "stage-2-topic-12-section-11"
      },
      {
        "title": "Production Secret Management Lab and Security Validation",
        "content": "A practical repository secret management lab should begin with a non-production repository and a test credential that can be safely revoked. Create a repository secret with a clear name, configure a workflow to access it through the secrets context, and pass it to only the step that requires it. Verify availability without printing the secret value. Next, create a separate staging environment and configure an environment-scoped secret with an approval requirement. Confirm that a deployment job cannot access the protected environment before approval and can proceed after the required reviewer authorizes it. Add a test that checks whether confidential values appear in workflow logs, generated artifacts, container images, or repository files. Review the workflow's GITHUB_TOKEN permissions and ensure untrusted pull-request events cannot execute privileged deployment steps. Simulate a credential rotation by updating the test secret, running the workflow again, and revoking the old test credential. Finally, document the owner, scope, usage, and rotation procedure. Production readiness requires more than a successful workflow run: verify least-privilege access, safe logging, correct environment boundaries, credential lifecycle handling, and a clear incident response process.",
        "id": "stage-2-topic-12-section-12"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "topicTitle": "Tags and Releases",
    "sections": [
      {
        "title": "Git Tags Fundamentals and Release Identification",
        "content": "A Git tag is a named reference that identifies a specific commit in a repository's history. Tags are commonly used to mark software release points such as v1.0.0, v2.3.1, or v3.0.0. Unlike a branch, which normally moves forward as new commits are added, a tag is intended to identify a fixed point in history. This makes tags useful for connecting source code to a published application version, container image, package, or deployment record. Developers can inspect existing tags with git tag and view the commit associated with a tag using git show. Tags help teams answer important operational questions: which source revision produced a release, which code should be deployed again, and which commit introduced a production defect? A release process should create tags only after the required validation and approval steps have completed. Tag names should follow a documented convention so that humans and automation can interpret them consistently. Teams should also protect release tags against unauthorized movement or deletion because changing a published release reference can damage traceability and create supply-chain confusion.",
        "id": "stage-2-topic-13-section-1"
      },
      {
        "title": "Lightweight Tags, Annotated Tags, and Signed Tags",
        "content": "Git supports lightweight and annotated tags. A lightweight tag is essentially a named reference to a commit and is useful for temporary or simple local markers. An annotated tag stores additional metadata, including the tagger, date, message, and target object. For software releases, annotated tags are generally preferable because they provide a meaningful record of why the tag was created and who created it. The command git tag -a v1.2.0 -m \"Release v1.2.0\" creates an annotated tag. A signed tag adds a cryptographic signature using a configured signing mechanism, allowing consumers to verify that the tag was created by a trusted signer and has not been altered. Teams can inspect tag details with git show v1.2.0 and verify signatures with git tag -v v1.2.0 when signing is configured. Signing is especially valuable for security-sensitive projects and software distributed to external users. A tag's metadata and signature do not replace release approval, protected access, or artifact verification. Organizations should define which tag types are permitted, who can create release tags, and how signing identities are managed.",
        "id": "stage-2-topic-13-section-2"
      },
      {
        "title": "Semantic Versioning and Version Naming Conventions",
        "content": "Semantic Versioning provides a widely used convention for communicating the compatibility impact of software changes. A version normally follows MAJOR.MINOR.PATCH, such as 2.4.1. The major component increases when incompatible public API changes are introduced, the minor component increases when backward-compatible functionality is added, and the patch component increases for backward-compatible bug fixes. Pre-release identifiers can represent versions such as 2.5.0-alpha.1 or 2.5.0-rc.1, while build metadata can identify a build without changing version precedence. Teams should define what constitutes a public interface for their application or library before applying these rules. A version number should not be increased randomly or independently by multiple automation jobs. Release automation can derive versions from approved tags, commit metadata, or a controlled versioning process. Consistent versioning helps package consumers understand upgrade risk, allows deployment systems to identify exact software versions, and improves incident investigation. Semantic Versioning is a convention rather than an automatic guarantee: a team must still test compatibility and document breaking changes accurately.",
        "id": "stage-2-topic-13-section-3"
      },
      {
        "title": "Creating, Listing, Pushing, and Deleting Git Tags",
        "content": "Git provides commands for creating and managing tags locally and on remote repositories. The command git tag lists local tags, while git tag -n displays tag names with their annotation messages. To create an annotated release tag, use git tag -a v1.4.0 -m \"Release v1.4.0\". To inspect it, run git show v1.4.0. A tag can be pushed explicitly with git push origin v1.4.0, or all local tags can be pushed using git push origin --tags. Explicitly pushing a release tag is often safer because it avoids publishing unrelated local tags unintentionally. To remove a local tag, use git tag -d v1.4.0. Removing a remote tag requires a separate remote operation, such as git push origin --delete v1.4.0, and should be restricted because consumers may already depend on that release identifier. Moving or recreating an existing published release tag is dangerous: different users may associate the same version name with different commits. Teams should create immutable release tags where possible and follow a documented correction procedure if a tag was published incorrectly.",
        "id": "stage-2-topic-13-section-4"
      },
      {
        "title": "GitHub Releases and Release Publication Lifecycle",
        "content": "A GitHub Release is a repository-level publication associated with a Git tag. It provides a human-readable description of a software version and can include release notes, downloadable assets, and pre-release or latest-release indicators. A release normally starts from an existing tag or a tag created during the publication process. The release page gives users a convenient location to find version information and download associated packages. A release lifecycle should begin only after the source revision has passed required tests, security checks, and release approval. Teams can prepare a draft release, review its notes and assets, and publish it when authorized. A pre-release can communicate that a version is intended for testing rather than general production use. A published release should identify the exact source tag and avoid ambiguous descriptions such as \"latest fixes.\" Release descriptions should explain major changes, compatibility considerations, known issues, and upgrade instructions. For production software, the release record should also connect to the build pipeline, artifact digest, approval evidence, and deployment history so that operators can verify exactly what was distributed.",
        "id": "stage-2-topic-13-section-5"
      },
      {
        "title": "Release Notes, Changelogs, and Change Communication",
        "content": "Release notes communicate what changed between software versions and what users or operators need to know before upgrading. A changelog is a maintained record of notable changes over time, while release notes usually focus on one specific release and its practical impact. Useful release notes can group changes into features, improvements, bug fixes, security updates, deprecations, and breaking changes. They should include relevant issue or pull-request references where appropriate, but avoid exposing confidential incident details or security-sensitive implementation information. Teams can generate draft notes from merged pull requests, labels, commit history, or release automation, but generated content should be reviewed for accuracy and clarity. A good release note explains user-visible behavior rather than simply listing internal commit messages. For infrastructure and platform releases, it should mention configuration changes, database migration requirements, dependency upgrades, operational impact, and rollback considerations. The release process should define who reviews and approves release notes. Accurate communication reduces upgrade mistakes, helps support teams answer questions, and provides a historical record for engineers investigating when a behavior changed.",
        "id": "stage-2-topic-13-section-6"
      },
      {
        "title": "Release Assets, Checksums, and Artifact Traceability",
        "content": "Release assets are files attached to a GitHub Release, such as compiled binaries, source archives, installation packages, checksums, and supporting documentation. These assets should be generated by a controlled build pipeline from the commit identified by the release tag. A release process should avoid manually uploading an unverified file from an engineer's workstation because this breaks reproducibility and weakens traceability. Checksums such as SHA-256 allow consumers to verify that a downloaded file matches the published digest, although a checksum hosted beside the file does not independently prove who produced it. Stronger supply-chain practices can include signed artifacts, provenance attestations, trusted build identities, and verification instructions. Artifact names should include the application and version, and platform-specific packages should be clearly distinguished. The pipeline should record the source commit, build run, toolchain versions, dependency inputs, artifact digest, and publication destination. Retention and access controls should be applied to build outputs and release assets. When a release is promoted across environments, deploy the same verified artifact rather than rebuilding a different package from the same source tag.",
        "id": "stage-2-topic-13-section-7"
      },
      {
        "title": "Release Tag Protection and Publication Permissions",
        "content": "Release tags are important supply-chain references and should be protected from unauthorized creation, deletion, or movement. A repository can use rulesets or supported tag protection controls to restrict which users, teams, or automation identities can create matching tag names. Organizations should define a predictable naming pattern such as v* for release tags and ensure that only approved release processes can publish them. Protection must cover both human actions and automation tokens because a workflow with excessive write permissions could modify release references. GitHub repository rules and rulesets provide controls whose exact capabilities depend on repository configuration and plan availability. Teams should review bypass permissions carefully and avoid granting broad exceptions for convenience. A release tag should point to the reviewed commit, and the associated release should be traceable to the approval that authorized publication. If an incorrect tag must be corrected, follow a documented incident or release-correction process and communicate the change to consumers. Do not silently move a published version tag to a different commit. Protecting release references preserves trust in version identifiers and makes deployments reproducible.",
        "id": "stage-2-topic-13-section-8"
      },
      {
        "title": "Automating Versioning and Release Creation with GitHub Actions",
        "content": "GitHub Actions can automate release preparation and publication when a repository follows a consistent versioning process. A workflow may run after a reviewed change is merged, when a release tag is pushed, or when an authorized user starts a manual release workflow. The pipeline can validate the version, run tests, build the application, generate release notes, create checksums, and publish approved assets. A tag-triggered workflow should verify that the tag points to the expected commit and that required validation has completed. Release automation should use narrowly scoped GITHUB_TOKEN permissions or a dedicated approved identity, granting contents: write only to the job that needs to create a release or upload assets. Third-party actions should be reviewed and pinned to trusted commit SHAs where practical. The workflow should not publish a release merely because an arbitrary tag was pushed by an unauthorized contributor. Use protected tags, branch policies, environment approvals, and explicit release conditions to control publication. A reliable automated release process produces repeatable artifacts, consistent notes, auditable workflow runs, and a clear relationship between the source commit and the published version.",
        "id": "stage-2-topic-13-section-9"
      },
      {
        "title": "Pre-Releases, Release Candidates, and Hotfix Versions",
        "content": "Software teams often need release identifiers that distinguish testing builds from stable production releases. A pre-release such as 3.0.0-alpha.1 can communicate that a version is still under development. A beta version may be shared with a wider testing group, while a release candidate such as 3.0.0-rc.1 indicates that the team believes the version may be ready if no blocking defects are found. These identifiers should follow a documented policy so users understand their stability expectations. A hotfix is a targeted change intended to correct an urgent issue in an already released version. The team should identify the affected release line, apply the fix through an approved branch or patch process, run focused and regression tests, and publish a new patch version. For example, a production defect in 2.4.1 may result in a 2.4.2 release if the change is backward-compatible. The hotfix should also be integrated into the main development line so the defect does not return in a future release. Release notes should explain the issue and any required upgrade action without exposing sensitive vulnerability details prematurely.",
        "id": "stage-2-topic-13-section-10"
      },
      {
        "title": "Release Rollback, Recovery, and Historical Version Reproduction",
        "content": "A release rollback restores a service to a previously known-good version or reverses a problematic change. A Git tag helps identify the source revision, but a tag alone does not guarantee that the old application can be redeployed. The corresponding build artifact, dependency versions, configuration, database compatibility, and deployment instructions must also be available. Production release processes should retain immutable artifacts and record their digests so operators can select a verified prior version. A rollback plan must consider database migrations, persistent data changes, external API compatibility, and infrastructure modifications because reverting application code may not reverse those changes safely. Some releases require a forward fix instead of a rollback, especially when a database migration is irreversible or older application versions cannot read the updated data. Teams should test recovery procedures before an incident and document the exact deployment command or workflow used to restore a previous artifact. After recovery, investigate the failed release, preserve relevant logs, and update the release process. Reproducible historical releases reduce recovery time and improve confidence during production incidents.",
        "id": "stage-2-topic-13-section-11"
      },
      {
        "title": "Production Release Management Lab and Verification",
        "content": "A practical release management lab should begin with a repository containing a small application and a working CI workflow. Create a reviewed change, run the required checks, and identify the exact commit intended for release. Create an annotated tag such as v1.0.0 and push that tag to the remote repository. Configure a release workflow that validates the tag, builds the application, runs tests, generates a checksum, and prepares a draft GitHub Release. Review the release notes, asset names, source commit, and workflow permissions before publishing. If the repository supports the required controls, configure a tag ruleset that restricts release tag creation to approved identities. Publish a pre-release first if the software requires wider validation, then follow the documented approval process for the stable release. Verify that users can identify the release tag, download the intended asset, and validate its checksum. Finally, simulate a rollback by selecting a previously retained artifact and deploying it to a non-production environment. The lab is complete only when the source tag, build output, published release, approval record, and recovery artifact can all be traced consistently.",
        "id": "stage-2-topic-13-section-12"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "topicTitle": "Release Approval Workflow",
    "sections": [
      {
        "title": "Release Approval Fundamentals and Governance Objectives",
        "content": "A release approval workflow is a controlled process that determines whether a software change is authorized to move into a target environment. It introduces explicit decision points between completing engineering validation and performing a deployment. The purpose is not simply to add a manual button; it is to ensure that the correct release, evidence, people, and operational conditions are reviewed before production changes occur. An approval process may require successful automated tests, security scans, code review, change documentation, deployment readiness checks, and confirmation that the selected artifact is the intended version. Approval responsibilities should be defined by organizational policy, with clear separation between developers, reviewers, release managers, and production operators where required. The workflow should record who authorized the release, what version was approved, when the decision occurred, and which environment was targeted. Approval rules must be proportionate to risk: routine low-risk changes can use streamlined controls, while high-impact infrastructure or security changes may require additional review. A well-designed process improves accountability without creating unnecessary delays or encouraging teams to bypass controls.",
        "id": "stage-2-topic-14-section-1"
      },
      {
        "title": "Release Readiness Criteria and Pre-Approval Validation Gates",
        "content": "A release should reach an approval stage only after its readiness criteria have been evaluated. Typical gates include successful compilation, unit and integration tests, code-quality checks, dependency analysis, container image scanning, infrastructure validation, and successful deployment to a lower environment. The release candidate should have a unique version or immutable artifact digest so that approvers know exactly what they are authorizing. Teams should define mandatory checks for each application instead of relying on a generic statement that the build passed. For example, a database-backed service may require migration validation, while a Kubernetes deployment may require manifest validation and resource compatibility checks. The workflow should fail closed when a mandatory gate fails or its result cannot be determined. Optional informational checks should be clearly distinguished from blocking checks. Evidence should be accessible from the approval record, including links to workflow runs, test reports, security results, change tickets, and deployment plans. A readiness checklist reduces subjective decisions and helps approvers focus on operational risk rather than manually repeating automated engineering checks.",
        "id": "stage-2-topic-14-section-2"
      },
      {
        "title": "Approver Roles, Responsibilities, and Separation of Duties",
        "content": "Release approval roles should reflect the risk and ownership of the system being changed. A technical approver may confirm that the implementation and tests are acceptable, a service owner may evaluate customer impact, a security reviewer may assess security findings, and a release manager may confirm that the release process is complete. Production operations teams may be responsible for deployment execution or post-deployment verification. Separation of duties reduces the possibility that one person can independently develop, approve, and deploy a sensitive change without oversight. However, the exact separation requirements depend on organizational policy, team size, and regulatory obligations. Small teams may use peer approval or additional automated evidence where strict role separation is impractical. Approval groups should have named ownership, documented responsibilities, and a process for handling unavailable approvers. Access should be reviewed periodically so that former employees or people who changed roles do not retain unnecessary approval authority. Approver selection must be based on authorization and system responsibility, not simply on who happens to be available to click an approval control.",
        "id": "stage-2-topic-14-section-3"
      },
      {
        "title": "GitHub Environments and Required Reviewer Protection",
        "content": "GitHub Actions environments provide deployment boundaries that can be associated with protection rules and environment-specific secrets. A deployment job can declare an environment such as staging or production, allowing configured reviewers and other supported protection requirements to control access. Required reviewers can be used to pause a deployment until an authorized person approves it. Branch restrictions can limit which branches or tags are permitted to deploy to an environment, and wait timers can introduce a deliberate delay before deployment becomes available. Environment secrets are made available to the protected job according to the environment's rules, helping separate production credentials from ordinary validation jobs. Repository administrators should configure environment access deliberately and verify the behavior with test deployments before relying on it for critical releases. Environment protection capabilities and limits can depend on repository visibility and GitHub plan. A workflow should not assume that defining an environment name automatically creates an approval requirement; the protection rules must be configured. Reviewers should be able to inspect the release version, workflow run, and relevant evidence before authorizing deployment.",
        "id": "stage-2-topic-14-section-4"
      },
      {
        "title": "Approval Workflow Design Using GitHub Actions Jobs and Dependencies",
        "content": "A GitHub Actions approval workflow can be structured as separate validation, packaging, staging, approval-gated deployment, and production verification jobs. The needs property defines which jobs must complete before another job can start. A production deployment job can declare a protected environment and wait for its configured reviewers before accessing the environment. The workflow should pass an immutable artifact reference from the build stage to deployment rather than rebuilding the application after approval. This ensures that the approved candidate is the same artifact that is deployed. Job conditions should be explicit and should not accidentally allow a failed validation job to be bypassed. The workflow should also handle skipped jobs and cancellation carefully because these states can affect downstream conditions. Use meaningful job names and summaries so approvers can understand the release state. Avoid implementing approval solely through an informal comment or an unverified external flag. A reliable design uses GitHub's supported environment protection features or an approved change-management integration, with clear job dependencies and auditable execution records.",
        "id": "stage-2-topic-14-section-5"
      },
      {
        "title": "Change Requests, Release Evidence, and Approval Context",
        "content": "Approvers need enough information to make a meaningful release decision. A change request should identify the application, version, target environment, planned deployment window, business purpose, and responsible release owner. It should link to the relevant pull requests, issue references, test results, security findings, build run, and deployment plan. Operational evidence may include service dependencies, expected impact, database migration details, monitoring dashboards, and recovery instructions. The evidence should identify the exact artifact digest or immutable package reference so that the approval cannot be confused with a different build. A release summary should explain what changed, why the change is needed, and whether any known limitations remain. Risk assessment should consider customer impact, blast radius, reversibility, dependency changes, and the consequences of failure. Avoid placing passwords, access tokens, private keys, or other sensitive values in approval descriptions and tickets. Evidence should be concise enough to review but complete enough to support a defensible decision. Standardizing the information presented to approvers reduces repeated questions and improves consistency across releases.",
        "id": "stage-2-topic-14-section-6"
      },
      {
        "title": "Approval Decisions, Rejection, Cancellation, and Resubmission",
        "content": "An approval workflow should define how approved, rejected, cancelled, expired, and resubmitted releases are handled. Approval authorizes the specific release candidate and environment that were reviewed; it should not be interpreted as unrestricted permission for later changes. If an approver rejects a release, the reason should be recorded and routed to the responsible engineering team. The team should correct the issue, rerun the required checks, and submit the updated candidate for a new decision. A materially changed artifact should not reuse an earlier approval unless organizational policy explicitly permits it and the approved artifact identity remains unchanged. Cancellation may be necessary when the deployment window closes, a production incident begins, a dependency becomes unavailable, or new risk information appears. A cancelled deployment should leave a clear record of why it stopped and whether a new approval is required. Avoid workflows that automatically retry a rejected production deployment or interpret an absent response as approval. Clear state transitions prevent ambiguity and ensure that deployment authorization remains tied to the exact evidence and release version that were evaluated.",
        "id": "stage-2-topic-14-section-7"
      },
      {
        "title": "Deployment Windows, Scheduling, and Release Coordination",
        "content": "A deployment window is an agreed period during which a release can be performed with suitable operational coverage and acceptable business impact. Teams may schedule releases to avoid peak customer activity, maintenance freezes, major business events, or periods when essential support staff are unavailable. A release approval workflow should record the intended window and verify that the release is still authorized when deployment begins. An approval obtained days earlier may no longer be appropriate if the artifact, risk profile, dependencies, or operating conditions have changed. Scheduling can be implemented through a release-management system, manual dispatch, or controlled workflow inputs, but the selected time must not bypass required validation and authorization. Teams should coordinate application deployments with infrastructure changes, database migrations, network updates, and dependent services. Concurrency controls can prevent multiple deployments from modifying the same environment simultaneously. If a release misses its approved window, the workflow should stop or require renewed authorization according to policy. Good coordination reduces collisions, improves support readiness, and makes production changes more predictable.",
        "id": "stage-2-topic-14-section-8"
      },
      {
        "title": "Emergency Releases, Break-Glass Approval, and Exception Handling",
        "content": "Emergency releases are changes required to address urgent incidents, actively exploited vulnerabilities, severe service degradation, or other time-critical risks. Emergency procedures may shorten normal review timelines, but they should not remove accountability or eliminate essential safety checks. Organizations should define who can authorize an emergency deployment, what conditions qualify, which minimum validations remain mandatory, and how the decision is documented. A break-glass process provides a controlled exception for situations where normal approval routes are unavailable or too slow. Access to emergency deployment authority should be restricted, monitored, and reviewed after use. Where possible, emergency changes should still use a reviewed commit, automated build, immutable artifact, and a traceable deployment record. If a specific test cannot be completed because of the urgency, the exception and its risk must be explicitly recorded, with a plan to complete validation afterward. Post-deployment review should confirm service health, examine the change, and identify whether additional corrective work is required. Emergency approval must not become a routine shortcut for poor planning or repeated bypassing of release controls.",
        "id": "stage-2-topic-14-section-9"
      },
      {
        "title": "Approval Audit Trails, Compliance, and Access Reviews",
        "content": "An approval audit trail records the decision-making and deployment events associated with a release. Important evidence includes the release identifier, source commit, artifact digest, target environment, approver identity, approval time, decision outcome, workflow run, and deployment result. GitHub workflow history and environment deployment records can provide part of this evidence, while an external change-management system may hold additional business approvals or compliance documentation. Organizations should understand the retention and audit capabilities available in their GitHub plan and configure any required external recordkeeping accordingly. Audit records should be protected against unauthorized alteration and retained according to applicable organizational policy. Periodic access reviews should verify that only authorized users and teams can approve production deployments. Review bypass permissions, environment administrators, repository roles, and automation identities as part of the process. Compliance evidence is most useful when it is generated automatically and linked to the actual deployment rather than assembled manually long after the event. A strong audit trail supports investigations, internal reviews, and demonstrations that required controls operated for a specific release.",
        "id": "stage-2-topic-14-section-10"
      },
      {
        "title": "Post-Approval Deployment Monitoring and Rollback Readiness",
        "content": "Approval is only one part of a safe release; the deployment must also be monitored and verified after authorization. A production workflow should capture the deployment result and run appropriate smoke tests, health checks, endpoint checks, and application-specific validation. Monitoring should examine service availability, error rates, latency, resource consumption, and critical business indicators. Teams should compare these signals against known baselines and alert thresholds rather than relying only on a successful deployment command. The release plan should identify rollback or recovery actions, the previous known-good artifact, and any database or infrastructure changes that complicate reversal. If post-deployment checks fail, the workflow should stop further rollout and notify the responsible team. Automated rollback should be used only when the recovery action is safe, tested, and compatible with the system's data and dependency state. Some failures require a forward fix or manual incident response instead of an automatic rollback. Record the deployed version and verification evidence so operators can quickly determine what changed. Release approval should confirm that monitoring and recovery plans exist before production deployment begins.",
        "id": "stage-2-topic-14-section-11"
      },
      {
        "title": "Production Release Approval Lab and End-to-End Verification",
        "content": "A practical release approval lab should use a sample repository with a working CI pipeline and separate staging and production environments. Configure the workflow to run automated validation, build a versioned artifact, and deploy that artifact to staging. Add a production deployment job that depends on successful validation and staging verification. Configure the production environment with an authorized required reviewer and appropriate branch restrictions. Trigger the workflow and confirm that the production job waits for approval rather than immediately deploying. Review the release summary, source commit, artifact identity, test evidence, and target environment before approving the deployment. Verify that the same artifact tested in staging is used in production. Repeat the exercise with a failed validation result and confirm that production deployment cannot proceed. Test a rejected or cancelled release and verify that the workflow records the decision without silently retrying deployment. Finally, inspect the workflow run and environment deployment history to confirm that the approver, decision, version, and deployment result are traceable. The lab is complete when authorization, evidence, deployment, verification, and recovery expectations operate as one controlled release process.",
        "id": "stage-2-topic-14-section-12"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "topicTitle": "Troubleshooting",
    "sections": [
      {
        "title": "Git and GitHub Troubleshooting Methodology",
        "content": "Effective Git and GitHub troubleshooting starts with understanding the observed failure rather than immediately running commands that change repository state. First identify the affected repository, branch, commit, user or automation identity, operation, and time when the issue occurred. Capture the exact error message and determine whether the problem is local to one workstation, affects multiple contributors, or occurs only on GitHub. Check the current repository state with git status, inspect recent history with git log, and verify the remote configuration with git remote -v. Before attempting recovery, preserve uncommitted work and create a backup or temporary branch when appropriate. Separate symptoms from root causes: a rejected push may be caused by authentication, branch protection, non-fast-forward history, or insufficient permissions, and each requires a different correction. Avoid destructive commands such as reset --hard, clean -fd, or force push until the impact is understood. Record the command, result, repository state, and recovery action so another engineer can reproduce the diagnosis. A disciplined process reduces accidental data loss and makes recurring repository problems easier to prevent.",
        "id": "stage-2-topic-15-section-1"
      },
      {
        "title": "Git Authentication, SSH, HTTPS, and Credential Failures",
        "content": "Authentication failures commonly occur when Git cannot prove the identity of the user or automation process accessing a remote repository. For HTTPS access, verify the configured remote URL and ensure the credential manager is using a valid supported authentication method. GitHub no longer accepts ordinary account passwords for Git operations over HTTPS; a personal access token or another supported authentication method may be required. For SSH access, inspect the remote URL, confirm that the correct private key is available to the SSH agent, and test connectivity with ssh -T git@github.com. Check file permissions on private keys and confirm that the corresponding public key is registered with the correct account or deploy-key configuration. An error such as Permission denied (publickey) indicates that SSH authentication did not succeed, but it does not by itself prove that the repository permissions are correct. If authentication succeeds but repository access is denied, inspect repository membership, organization policy, SSO authorization, token scope, and deploy-key restrictions. Never paste private keys or tokens into logs or troubleshooting messages. Rotate exposed credentials and remove obsolete credentials from the credential manager after confirming the replacement works.",
        "id": "stage-2-topic-15-section-2"
      },
      {
        "title": "Repository Access, Permission Denied, and Remote Configuration",
        "content": "Repository access problems can result from an incorrect remote URL, missing repository membership, insufficient role permissions, organization restrictions, or an authentication identity that differs from the expected user. Begin with git remote -v and verify the repository owner, name, and protocol. Confirm that the repository still exists and that the user or automation identity is authorized to access it. On GitHub, review repository roles, team access, organization membership, outside-collaborator permissions, and any applicable SSO or enterprise policies. A user may be able to clone a repository but lack permission to push changes or administer settings. Similarly, a workflow may have read access but not the write permission required to publish a release. For GitHub Actions, inspect the workflow's GITHUB_TOKEN permissions and organization-level policy before changing credentials. Avoid solving an access problem by granting broad administrator permissions. Assign the minimum role required for the task and verify the exact operation again. If access was recently changed, check whether the user is using an old credential or a different account. Record the approved access change and remove temporary elevated permissions after the task is complete.",
        "id": "stage-2-topic-15-section-3"
      },
      {
        "title": "Branch Creation, Tracking, and Upstream Synchronization Issues",
        "content": "Branch-related errors often occur when a local branch does not track the intended remote branch or when the branch name differs from the one expected by the team. Use git branch -vv to inspect local branches and their upstream tracking relationships. Use git branch -r to inspect remote-tracking branches and git fetch origin to refresh the local view of remote references. If a local branch has no upstream, configure it with git push -u origin branch-name when publishing it for the first time. If Git reports that a branch does not exist on the remote, confirm the spelling, remote name, and whether the branch was deleted or renamed. A branch that appears behind its remote counterpart may simply need a fetch followed by a merge or rebase according to team policy. Do not assume that a local branch named main is automatically tracking origin/main. Before changing tracking configuration, inspect the current branch and its commit history. When a branch has been renamed, update local references and any dependent automation or pull-request configuration carefully. Consistent branch naming, documented upstream setup, and routine fetch operations prevent many synchronization problems.",
        "id": "stage-2-topic-15-section-4"
      },
      {
        "title": "Push Rejections, Non-Fast-Forward Errors, and Protected Branches",
        "content": "A non-fast-forward push rejection occurs when the remote branch contains commits that are not present in the local branch, so Git cannot update the remote reference without potentially discarding history. Fetch the latest remote changes and inspect the difference with git log --oneline --graph --decorate --all. Integrate the remote commits using the team's approved merge or rebase strategy, resolve conflicts if necessary, run tests, and push again. A different rejection may occur because the destination branch is protected. Protected branches can require pull requests, successful status checks, signed commits, or approval from authorized reviewers. In that case, pushing directly is not the correct recovery path; create or update a feature branch and submit a pull request that satisfies the repository rules. Do not use force push as a routine way to bypass a rejection. If a force update is genuinely required for a controlled branch, verify the impact with collaborators and use the safer --force-with-lease option only when authorized. Inspect server-side error messages and repository rules before changing local history. This distinction prevents engineers from treating policy enforcement and history divergence as the same technical problem.",
        "id": "stage-2-topic-15-section-5"
      },
      {
        "title": "Pull Request, Review, and Merge Blocker Diagnosis",
        "content": "A pull request may be blocked even when its code changes appear correct. Common causes include unresolved review comments, missing approvals, outdated branch requirements, failing status checks, merge conflicts, draft status, and restrictions on the contributor's permissions. Start by reviewing the pull-request conversation, Files changed, Checks, and mergeability indicators. Identify the exact required check that is failing and open its associated workflow run rather than relying only on the overall red status. If approval is missing, confirm the required reviewer rules and whether a new commit invalidated an earlier approval under the repository's settings. If the pull request is out of date with its base branch, update it using the repository's approved merge or rebase process and rerun validation. If GitHub reports a merge conflict, resolve it locally or through the supported interface, then verify the resulting diff before pushing. Do not dismiss reviews or bypass required checks simply to make a merge button available. Once all requirements are satisfied, confirm the intended merge strategy and verify that the resulting commit appears on the target branch.",
        "id": "stage-2-topic-15-section-6"
      },
      {
        "title": "Merge Conflicts, Incorrect History, and Lost Commit Recovery",
        "content": "Merge conflicts happen when Git cannot automatically reconcile competing changes, often because two branches modify the same lines or related file structures. Run git status to identify conflicted files and inspect conflict markers before editing them. Resolve each file according to the intended application behavior, remove conflict markers, stage the resolved files, and complete the merge or rebase using the appropriate continuation command. After resolution, inspect git diff and run tests because a syntactically clean merge can still introduce logical defects. If commits appear to be missing, inspect git log --all and git reflog to identify recent branch movements, resets, rebases, or checkouts. The reflog can help locate commits that are no longer referenced by a branch, but its entries are local and subject to expiration and cleanup. Create a recovery branch from a discovered commit before attempting further history changes. Avoid immediately running garbage collection or destructive cleanup commands. For a shared branch, coordinate with collaborators before rewriting history because their local references may depend on the previous commit sequence. A recovery procedure should preserve the original state, verify the restored history, and document any rewritten references.",
        "id": "stage-2-topic-15-section-7"
      },
      {
        "title": "GitHub Actions Workflow Failures and Runner Diagnostics",
        "content": "When a GitHub Actions workflow fails, begin with the workflow run summary and identify the first failing job and step. Read the complete relevant log context rather than focusing only on the final error line. Common causes include invalid YAML indentation, incorrect event filters, missing action inputs, unsupported runtime versions, unavailable runner labels, dependency installation failures, and insufficient token permissions. Confirm that the workflow file is located under .github/workflows and that the event which should trigger it matches the configured on filters. For runner-related issues, inspect runs-on labels, runner availability, job queue time, disk space, and required software. For dependency failures, inspect lockfiles, package registry access, cache keys, and runtime setup. For permission failures, review workflow-level and job-level permissions and the access restrictions associated with the triggering event. Use explicit timeouts and meaningful error messages to make failures easier to diagnose. Re-run only after identifying whether the failure is transient or deterministic. If a self-hosted runner is involved, check its service health and isolation without exposing credentials or sensitive workspace data.",
        "id": "stage-2-topic-15-section-8"
      },
      {
        "title": "Webhook Delivery Failures and Integration Debugging",
        "content": "GitHub webhook integrations can fail because the receiver is unavailable, returns an unsuccessful HTTP response, rejects the request signature, or cannot process the event payload. Start by inspecting the webhook delivery history in repository or organization settings and identify the response code, response body, delivery time, and endpoint URL. A connection timeout or server error may indicate network, DNS, TLS, load-balancer, or application availability problems. A client error may indicate incorrect endpoint routing, payload validation, or authentication handling. Verify that the receiver uses the expected content type and reads the raw request body when validating an HMAC signature. Signature verification should use the configured webhook secret and a constant-time comparison. Check whether the receiver acknowledges requests quickly and moves expensive processing to a queue or background worker. GitHub may retry failed deliveries in supported situations, so the receiver must tolerate duplicate event delivery using delivery identifiers or idempotent processing. Do not solve signature failures by disabling verification. Correct the secret configuration, request parsing, or endpoint logic and replay a controlled delivery after the fix.",
        "id": "stage-2-topic-15-section-9"
      },
      {
        "title": "GitHub Secrets, Token Permissions, and Security Troubleshooting",
        "content": "Secret and token failures can cause deployments, package publishing, and API operations to fail even when the workflow itself is valid. Verify that the secret name matches the workflow expression exactly and that the secret exists at the expected repository, organization, or environment scope. Environment secrets may not become available until the job passes the configured environment protection rules. Fork pull-request workflows and other untrusted execution contexts have restrictions that can prevent access to secrets or write permissions. Check the workflow's permissions block and grant only the specific token scopes required by the operation. For cloud deployment, inspect OIDC configuration, token audience, trust-policy conditions, and the permissions assigned to the temporary cloud role. Never print secret values to diagnose a missing credential; test presence safely and inspect authentication error codes instead. If a token or secret may have been exposed, revoke or rotate it and investigate relevant workflow logs and artifacts. Confirm that third-party actions are trusted and pinned appropriately. Security troubleshooting should restore the required functionality without broadening permissions or weakening protections.",
        "id": "stage-2-topic-15-section-10"
      },
      {
        "title": "Tags, Releases, and Deployment Failure Investigation",
        "content": "Release failures can occur when a tag points to an unexpected commit, release automation lacks write permission, an asset upload fails, or a deployment uses an incorrect artifact. Begin by inspecting the tag with git show and confirming the commit identifier against the approved release candidate. Verify that the tag was pushed to the expected remote and that repository rules allow the authorized release identity to create it. For GitHub Actions release workflows, inspect the triggering event, tag filters, GITHUB_TOKEN permissions, and action versions. If a release is created but an asset is missing, inspect the build output, artifact upload step, file paths, and retention settings. For deployment failures, verify the artifact digest, target environment, credentials, deployment configuration, and approval status. Do not move an existing published tag to another commit as a quick repair because consumers may already rely on the original reference. If a release must be withdrawn or corrected, follow the documented release correction procedure and communicate the replacement version clearly. Preserve the previous known-good artifact and verify rollback readiness before attempting another production deployment.",
        "id": "stage-2-topic-15-section-11"
      },
      {
        "title": "Incident Recovery, Root Cause Analysis, and Preventive Controls",
        "content": "A recurring Git or GitHub issue should be treated as an operational problem rather than repeatedly fixed through undocumented manual commands. After service or repository functionality has been restored, build a timeline of the failure, identify the triggering change, and collect relevant command output, workflow logs, audit evidence, and affected commit references. Separate the immediate technical cause from contributing factors such as unclear permissions, missing tests, unsafe automation, weak branch policies, or undocumented release procedures. Recovery actions should be verified through a repeatable test that demonstrates the original operation now succeeds. Preventive controls may include branch protection, required status checks, token permission limits, protected environments, webhook signature validation, automated secret rotation, and better runner monitoring. Document safe recovery commands and explicitly identify destructive operations that require approval. Assign owners and due dates to corrective actions so that root-cause analysis results in measurable improvements. For critical repositories, maintain a recovery runbook that covers access failures, branch recovery, failed workflows, release rollback, and compromised credentials. Review incidents periodically to identify patterns and improve the reliability of the entire development and delivery process.",
        "id": "stage-2-topic-15-section-12"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "topicTitle": "Production Hands-on Lab",
    "sections": [
      {
        "title": "Production Lab Architecture, Objectives, and Success Criteria",
        "content": "This production hands-on lab builds a complete Git and GitHub collaboration workflow for a software team. The exercise begins with a repository, establishes controlled contribution practices, validates changes through pull requests and automated checks, and finishes with a governed release process. Use a sample application repository containing application source code, automated tests, documentation, and a GitHub Actions workflow. Define the participants as repository administrator, developer, reviewer, and release approver so that responsibilities are clear. The target workflow should use a protected default branch, short-lived feature branches, mandatory pull-request reviews, and automated validation before merging. Establish measurable success criteria: developers can clone and contribute, unauthorized direct pushes are rejected, required checks execute successfully, conflicts can be resolved without losing work, and an approved release can be traced to a specific commit and artifact. Use a test repository rather than experimenting on an important production repository. Record the initial configuration, repository URL, branch names, and test identities. At the end, verify the full workflow from the first commit through the final release and retain evidence that each control behaved as expected.",
        "id": "stage-2-topic-16-section-1"
      },
      {
        "title": "Repository Initialization, Configuration, and Team Access Setup",
        "content": "Create a GitHub repository with a meaningful name, description, and appropriate visibility. Initialize the repository with a README and add a .gitignore that excludes generated files, local configuration, credentials, and other content that should not be version controlled. Clone the repository to the development workstation using the approved HTTPS or SSH authentication method. Configure the local Git identity with git config user.name and git config user.email, and verify the values before creating commits. Add the application source, test structure, and contribution documentation in a controlled initial commit. Inspect the repository using git status, git log, and git remote -v to confirm that the local working copy points to the intended remote. Configure repository collaborators or teams with the minimum roles required for their responsibilities. Keep administration separate from ordinary development and avoid granting write access to users who only need to review changes. Confirm that each participant can perform the intended operation and cannot perform unauthorized administrative actions. Document the repository setup, default branch, access model, and recovery owner. This establishes a reproducible starting point for the remaining exercises and prevents later failures caused by incorrect remotes, identities, or permissions.",
        "id": "stage-2-topic-16-section-2"
      },
      {
        "title": "Feature Branch Creation and Controlled Development Workflow",
        "content": "Implement a short-lived feature branch workflow for the sample application. Start by fetching the latest remote references and checking out the current default branch. Create a feature branch with a descriptive name such as feature/add-health-endpoint using git switch -c. Make a small application change and include a corresponding automated test so that the contribution has observable behavior. Review the working tree with git status and inspect the changes using git diff before staging. Stage only the intended files rather than automatically including generated output, local settings, or unrelated modifications. Create a meaningful commit message that explains the change and its purpose. Push the feature branch to the remote and configure upstream tracking with git push -u origin branch-name. Verify the remote branch exists and that the commit is visible in GitHub. Repeat the exercise with a second developer branch to understand how multiple contributors work independently. Do not develop directly on the protected default branch. Keep feature branches focused and small so that reviews are easier and conflicts are less likely. The expected result is a traceable feature branch containing a deliberate change and its associated test, ready for pull-request review.",
        "id": "stage-2-topic-16-section-3"
      },
      {
        "title": "Commit Quality, History Inspection, and Change Traceability",
        "content": "Use the lab repository to establish a commit history that is easy to understand and audit. Each commit should represent a logical unit of work rather than combining unrelated changes. Before committing, inspect staged changes with git diff --cached and confirm that no secrets, local environment files, debug output, or generated artifacts are included. Use concise imperative commit subjects and add explanatory body text when the reason for a change is not obvious. Inspect history with git log --oneline --graph --decorate and compare branches to understand how feature work relates to the default branch. Use git show commit-id to review an individual change and git diff base-branch...feature-branch to inspect the complete pull-request change set. If a commit contains an accidental file, correct it safely before publishing when possible; if it has already been shared, coordinate with collaborators before rewriting history. For a sensitive credential accidentally committed, removing the file in a later commit is not sufficient: revoke or rotate the credential and follow an approved history-cleanup procedure. The exercise is complete when another engineer can identify what changed, who contributed it, why it was needed, and which pull request introduced it.",
        "id": "stage-2-topic-16-section-4"
      },
      {
        "title": "Pull Request Creation, Description, and Automated Validation",
        "content": "Open a pull request from the feature branch into the protected default branch. Give it a descriptive title and include the problem being solved, implementation summary, test evidence, risk assessment, and any deployment considerations. Link the related issue or work item when one exists. Review the Files changed tab and confirm that the pull request contains only the intended modifications. Add the appropriate reviewers and apply repository labels that communicate the change type or operational risk. Ensure that the pull request triggers the expected GitHub Actions validation workflow. Inspect the Checks tab and confirm that build, unit tests, static analysis, and any required security checks execute against the proposed change. If a check fails, inspect its job logs, identify the first meaningful error, fix the cause on the feature branch, and push the correction. Verify that the new commit triggers the expected validation again. Do not treat a green workflow as proof that the change is correct without reviewing the actual test coverage and scope. The expected outcome is a pull request with a clear description, limited diff, appropriate reviewers, and successful required automated checks.",
        "id": "stage-2-topic-16-section-5"
      },
      {
        "title": "Peer Review, Review Feedback, and Approval Validation",
        "content": "Perform a structured code review using a separate reviewer identity. The reviewer should inspect correctness, readability, test quality, error handling, security implications, performance impact, and compatibility with existing application behavior. Review the complete diff rather than relying only on the pull-request summary. Add inline comments for specific concerns and explain the reason for each requested change. The developer should respond to feedback, update the feature branch, and ensure that the new commits are included in the same pull request. After changes are pushed, the reviewer must inspect the updated code and verify that previous concerns have been addressed. Where repository rules require approval from a designated team or code owner, confirm that the approval comes from an eligible reviewer. Test how the repository handles approval invalidation when new commits are pushed if that setting is enabled. Avoid approving your own changes when separation of duties is required. The lab should demonstrate that review is a meaningful quality and risk-control step, not merely a button click. Retain the review conversation and approval status as evidence of the completed collaboration process.",
        "id": "stage-2-topic-16-section-6"
      },
      {
        "title": "Branch Protection Rules and Required Repository Controls",
        "content": "Configure branch protection or the repository ruleset for the default branch. Require pull requests before merging and define the minimum number of approvals appropriate for the exercise. Require the relevant GitHub Actions status checks and configure whether branches must be up to date before merging. Where appropriate, require conversation resolution, signed commits, or restrictions on who can push to the protected branch. Prevent force pushes and branch deletion for the protected branch unless an explicitly governed exception is necessary. Confirm that administrators and automation identities are subject to the intended rules or document any deliberate bypass configuration. Test the controls using a contributor account: attempt a direct push and verify that GitHub rejects it, then open a pull request and confirm that merging remains blocked until required approvals and checks are complete. Do not weaken production rules merely to make the lab pass. Record the rule configuration and the observed enforcement results. This exercise demonstrates that repository policy is enforced by the platform rather than depending only on developers remembering informal team conventions.",
        "id": "stage-2-topic-16-section-7"
      },
      {
        "title": "Merge Strategy Selection and Conflict Resolution Exercise",
        "content": "Select a merge strategy that matches the team's history and traceability requirements. Use a test pull request to compare merge commits, squash merging, and rebase merging, while recognizing that the repository may allow only a subset of these options. A merge commit preserves the branch integration event, squash merging combines the pull-request changes into one commit, and rebase merging replays commits onto the target branch. Explain the selected approach in the team's contribution guidance so that developers use a consistent method. To practise conflict resolution, create two feature branches that modify the same lines in a shared file. Merge one change first, then update the second branch and observe the conflict. Fetch the latest default branch, integrate it using the team's approved method, and inspect the conflict markers carefully. Resolve the file based on the intended application behavior, stage the result, and complete the merge or rebase. Run tests and inspect the final diff before pushing. Confirm that no unrelated changes or conflict markers remain. This exercise teaches developers to resolve competing changes deliberately while preserving a reviewable and correct result.",
        "id": "stage-2-topic-16-section-8"
      },
      {
        "title": "GitHub Actions CI Pipeline and Repository Automation",
        "content": "Create a GitHub Actions workflow under .github/workflows that validates pull requests and changes to the default branch. Use explicit workflow triggers, supported runner labels, and separate jobs for build and test when that structure improves clarity. Check out the repository, configure the required language runtime, install dependencies using the project's lockfile, and execute the build and automated test commands. Use dependency caching only when the cache key correctly reflects the dependency files and runtime configuration. Define job permissions explicitly and grant only the access required by each job. Add concurrency controls when duplicate runs for the same pull request or branch would waste resources. Configure timeouts and make failure output understandable. Test the workflow by opening a pull request with a deliberate failing test, confirm that the required check becomes unsuccessful, then correct the test and verify a successful run. Inspect the workflow logs to understand job dependencies, runner execution, and artifact handling. The final pipeline should provide reliable feedback before merge and should not require broad repository permissions for ordinary build and test operations.",
        "id": "stage-2-topic-16-section-9"
      },
      {
        "title": "Repository Secrets, Environment Protection, and Secure Deployment Configuration",
        "content": "Configure the lab's sensitive deployment settings using GitHub repository, organization, or environment secrets as appropriate. Never commit credentials, access tokens, private keys, or production configuration values to the repository. Use placeholder values in sample configuration files and document the required secret names separately. For a deployment workflow, create a protected environment and configure required reviewers or other environment protection rules supported by the repository plan and settings. Reference secrets only in the job or step that needs them and avoid printing them in logs. Where cloud deployment supports OpenID Connect, prefer short-lived federated credentials over long-lived static access keys. Configure the cloud trust relationship to restrict which repository, branch, environment, or workflow identity can obtain credentials. Test the workflow with a non-production environment and verify that deployment cannot proceed before the required protection conditions are met. Review token permissions and ensure that pull requests from untrusted forks cannot access deployment secrets. Record secret ownership, rotation expectations, and emergency revocation steps without recording the secret values themselves.",
        "id": "stage-2-topic-16-section-10"
      },
      {
        "title": "Webhook Integration and External System Event Processing",
        "content": "Integrate the lab repository with a controlled test webhook receiver or an approved development endpoint. Subscribe only to the events required by the integration, such as pull-request, push, or release events. Configure a webhook secret and validate the request signature at the receiving service using the raw request body and a secure comparison method. Record delivery identifiers and event metadata while avoiding sensitive payload data in application logs. Make the receiver acknowledge requests promptly and process longer-running operations asynchronously. Implement idempotency so that duplicate deliveries do not create duplicate tickets, deployments, or notifications. Use GitHub's webhook delivery history to inspect the response status and troubleshoot failed deliveries. Simulate a receiver failure in the test environment and confirm that the integration can recover through the supported redelivery or replay procedure. Verify that invalid signatures are rejected and that unrelated event types are ignored. The result should be a secure, observable integration that reacts to repository activity without trusting arbitrary requests or creating duplicate downstream actions.",
        "id": "stage-2-topic-16-section-11"
      },
      {
        "title": "Release Tagging, Versioning, and Artifact Traceability",
        "content": "Prepare a release candidate only after the pull request has passed required checks and has been merged through the approved process. Choose a versioning convention such as Semantic Versioning when it matches the project's release model. Confirm the target commit on the default branch and create an annotated Git tag containing the release version and a meaningful message. Inspect the tag locally with git show and push it to the intended remote using the approved release procedure. Configure release automation to build from the tagged commit rather than from an arbitrary moving branch reference. Generate the release artifact, record its version and digest, and attach release notes that describe changes, compatibility, and known limitations. Verify that the release identity has only the permissions required to create the release and upload assets. Do not silently move a published tag to a different commit. If a release must be corrected, use a documented replacement or withdrawal process and communicate the change to consumers. The lab is successful when the release can be traced from its tag to the merged pull request, source commit, build run, and produced artifact.",
        "id": "stage-2-topic-16-section-12"
      },
      {
        "title": "Release Approval, Production Deployment, and Rollback Readiness",
        "content": "Create a controlled production deployment workflow that is triggered by an approved release event or an explicitly authorized deployment action. Separate build and deployment responsibilities so that the artifact is produced and validated before it reaches the production environment. Configure environment protection rules and required reviewers where supported. The deployment job should reference the exact approved artifact and version rather than rebuilding from an unverified branch state. Record the approver, deployment identity, release tag, commit identifier, workflow run, and target environment. Use a non-production environment first to validate the workflow and confirm that the production approval gate blocks unauthorized execution. Define health checks and post-deployment verification steps that confirm the application is functioning as expected. Prepare a rollback procedure that identifies the previous known-good artifact and the authorized method for restoring it. Do not assume that reverting a Git commit automatically reverses database or infrastructure changes; those require their own recovery plan. After deployment, verify service health, inspect logs and metrics, and record the deployment result. The lab is complete when approval, deployment, verification, and rollback readiness are demonstrated end to end.",
        "id": "stage-2-topic-16-section-13"
      },
      {
        "title": "Final Production Validation, Audit Evidence, and Lab Acceptance",
        "content": "Perform a final acceptance review of the entire repository workflow. Confirm that the repository has the intended default branch, team permissions, contribution guidance, and protected-branch rules. Verify that feature work was developed on separate branches, commits are understandable, pull requests contain focused changes, reviewers provided meaningful feedback, and required automated checks passed before merging. Confirm that the selected merge strategy was followed and that the final history can be traced. Inspect GitHub Actions workflow permissions, secret references, environment protections, and webhook configuration for unnecessary access or unsafe handling. Verify that the release tag points to the approved commit and that the published artifact can be associated with its build run. Review deployment approval evidence, health-check results, and rollback instructions. Capture the relevant pull-request links, commit identifiers, tag name, workflow run references, and configuration evidence without copying credentials or sensitive secret values. Record any failed test, corrective action, and remaining limitation. The lab should be accepted only when every required control has been tested and its result documented. Finish by publishing a short operational handover describing repository ownership, release responsibilities, incident escalation, and the process for maintaining these controls.",
        "id": "stage-2-topic-16-section-14"
      }
    ]
  }
]
